    "description": "Signaling by ERBB2 KD Mutants describes how point mutations and small insertions in the kinase domain of ERBB2 (HER2) convert a growth factor receptor into a constitutively active oncogenic driver. Such mutations are found in breast, lung, gastric and bladder cancers, often without gene amplification, and they define tumours that may respond to HER2-directed tyrosine kinase inhibitors, while secondary mutations at the gatekeeper threonine cause acquired resistance. Mutant ERBB2 heterodimerizes more readily with EGFR and ERBB3 in the presence of neuregulins and EGF-family ligands, increasing autophosphorylation and prolonging recruitment of GRB2, SHC1, SOS1 and GAB1 to drive RAS-ERK signalling, and of PI3K subunits to drive PI3K-AKT survival signalling, with PLCG1 as an additional effector. Folding and stability of the mutant receptor still depend on the HSP90:CDC37 chaperone system, and ERBIN restrains its localization. The net output is ligand-hypersensitive proliferation and cellular transformation."
