    "description": "NGF-stimulated transcription is the nuclear output of nerve growth factor signalling: the wave of gene expression that converts a transient kinase signal at the TrkA receptor into the lasting decision of a neuronal precursor to stop dividing and grow neurites. It supports sympathetic and sensory neuron differentiation and survival, and the same machinery underlies activity-dependent gene expression in mature neurons, so it links extracellular trophic cues to synaptic plasticity and memory. Sustained ERK signalling activates CREB, ELK1 and SRF, which drive immediate early genes including FOS, FOSB, JUNB and the EGR family; these encode transcription factors that in turn switch on delayed response genes such as VGF and TPH1, with EP300 and CHD4 shaping chromatin and REST and the ID proteins restraining neuronal programmes. CDK5 and its p35/p39 activators couple the response to cytoskeletal remodelling during neurite extension."
