    "description": "regulation of MAP kinase activity sets how strongly and how long cells respond to growth factors, cytokines and stress through the ERK, JNK and p38 cascades, which in turn dictate proliferation, differentiation, inflammatory gene expression and apoptosis. Because these kinases are shared by many stimuli, output specificity depends on this tier of control, and its loss underlies oncogenic growth signalling and chronic inflammation. Receptor tyrosine kinases such as FGFR1, ERBB2, PDGFRB and FLT3 feed in from the plasma membrane, while innate immune adaptors MYD88, IRAK1/4 and TRAF6 route TLR and TNF signals through MAP3K7 to stress kinases. Amplitude is tuned by scaffolds and pseudokinases including KSR1, MAPK8IP1 and the Tribbles proteins, by phosphatases such as DUSP7, PTPN1 and PTPRJ, and by ESCRT-dependent receptor sorting through HGS and TSG101, which terminates signalling by degrading activated receptors."
