    "description": "RUNX1 regulates transcription of genes involved in differentiation of myeloid cells describes how the master haematopoietic transcription factor RUNX1, acting with its obligate partner CBFB, directs myeloid progenitors toward mature granulocytes, macrophages, erythrocytes and platelet-producing megakaryocytes. This programme sets the output of adult blood production; disruption of RUNX1 or CBFB by translocation or point mutation blocks differentiation at the progenitor stage and is among the most common lesions in acute myeloid leukaemia and familial platelet disorder. Mechanistically the RUNX1:CBFB heterodimer binds target promoters and recruits the histone acetyltransferase CREBBP to activate transcription, inducing the growth factor GM-CSF (CSF2) that drives proliferation and survival of myeloid progenitors, the lectin galectin-3 whose levels rise through maturation, and protein kinase C-beta, which governs myeloid cell apoptosis. It links lineage-specifying transcription to cytokine signalling and survival control."
