    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "FGFR1 ligand binding and activation",
    "description": "FGFR1 ligand binding and activation is the first step in fibroblast growth factor signalling through receptor 1, the event that converts an extracellular FGF gradient into a receptor dimer poised to fire. It governs limb and craniofacial patterning, skeletal growth, olfactory bulb and GnRH neuron development, and phosphate homeostasis; loss-of-function mutations in FGFR1 or its partner anosmin cause Kallmann syndrome and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, while activating changes drive craniosynostosis and several cancers. Which of the paracrine FGFs binds is set by alternative splicing of the receptor's third immunoglobulin domain and by heparan sulfate, with anosmin and TGFBR3 acting as accessory co-receptors and GIPC1 shaping trafficking. The endocrine ligand FGF23 instead requires Klotho, linking the receptor to renal phosphate handling. Ligand capture here dictates the downstream RAS-MAPK, PI3K and PLC-gamma responses, and FGFR1 also reaches the nucleus to influence transcription directly."
