    "name": "Wnt signaling pathway",
    "description": "Wnt signaling pathway is the cell-cell communication system that patterns the embryo and sustains stem cell compartments in adult tissues such as the intestinal crypt, bone and hair follicle. Secreted Wnt ligands, lipid-modified by PORCN and exported via WLS, engage Frizzled receptors with LRP5/6 or alternative co-receptors including ROR1/2, RYK and PTK7. In the canonical branch this inactivates the AXIN-APC-GSK3-CK1 destruction complex so that beta-catenin accumulates, enters the nucleus and partners with TCF/LEF, displacing TLE corepressors to drive MYC, CCND1 and AXIN2. Non-canonical branches instead direct planar cell polarity and convergent extension through Dishevelled, VANGL, PRICKLE, CELSR and small GTPases. Tuning comes from DKK, SFRP, WIF1, NOTUM, and the RNF43/ZNRF3-RSPO-LGR module that sets receptor abundance. Loss of restraint, as in APC mutation, causes colorectal cancer, while insufficient signalling underlies bone density and limb patterning defects."
