    "name": "regulation of protein transport",
    "description": "regulation of protein transport covers the control layer over where proteins go — their export from the ER, transit through the Golgi, delivery to the surface, secretion, and import into the nucleus — rather than the transport machinery alone. Setting the rate and destination of protein movement is how cells time hormone release, present receptors at the membrane, and clear misfolded cargo, and its failure underlies diabetes, neurodegeneration and lipid storage disease. The genes span COPII budding and cargo selection, Rab11-dependent recycling endosomes, SNARE-mediated fusion, ERAD-linked retrotranslocation and quality control, nucleocytoplasmic shuttling via importins and exportins, and ESCRT sorting. A large regulatory contingent — kinases such as PKA, PKC and GSK3B, 14-3-3 adaptors, nuclear receptors, and microRNAs — tunes these steps to metabolic and inflammatory cues, with insulin granule exocytosis from pancreatic beta cells and neuronal vesicle release as prominent outputs."
