    "source": "go",
    "name": "obsolete peptidyl-serine modification",
    "description": "peptidyl-serine modification covers the covalent alteration of serine residues in proteins, overwhelmingly their phosphorylation by serine/threonine kinases, which is the most common switch cells use to change protein activity, localisation and stability within seconds of a stimulus. Reversible serine phosphorylation is what allows growth factor, cytokine and stress signals to be converted into decisions about proliferation, survival, metabolism and cell death, and its deregulation underlies much of oncogenesis. The genes span the major kinase families that carry out this chemistry: RAF-MEK and AKT/mTOR effectors downstream of receptor tyrosine kinases, casein kinase 2 and GSK3 as constitutive modifiers, DNA damage-responsive kinases such as ATM, PRKDC and SMG1, mitotic and polarity kinases including AURKA and BRSK1, and inflammatory kinases IKBKB, TBK1 and RIPK1. Counter-regulators such as PTEN and SPRY2, and cytokine inputs including IL6, LIF and IFNG, place the modification within signalling networks rather than a single route."
