    "description": "Receptor catabolic process is the controlled destruction of signalling receptors, the step that sets how long and how strongly a cell can respond to a hormone, growth factor, morphogen or neurotransmitter. Because a receptor that is not degraded keeps signalling, this pathway is a brake on proliferation and metabolic signalling: loss of the ubiquitin ligases RNF43 and ZNRF3 leaves Frizzled receptors on the surface and drives Wnt-dependent tumours, while PCSK9 and MYLIP direct the LDL receptor to lysosomal or ubiquitin-dependent turnover and thereby set plasma cholesterol. Mechanistically, HECT and RING ligases such as NEDD4, ITCH and SMURF1 mark activated receptors with ubiquitin; sorting adaptors, ESCRT components and KIF16B route them into multivesicular bodies; and lysosomal proteases including legumain and calpain, together with the autophagy regulators BECN1, UVRAG and PIK3R4, complete degradation. Hepcidin-driven ferroportin removal and furin-family processing extend the same logic to iron and receptor maturation."
