    "description": "Lytic vacuole organization covers the building, positioning, and remodelling of the cell's degradative compartment — in human cells, the lysosome and its relatives such as melanosomes and lytic granules. It matters because lysosomes are where macromolecules are broken down and recycled, where nutrient status is sensed, and where membrane traffic terminates; when their biogenesis fails the result is lysosomal storage disease, neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, or neurodegeneration. Mechanistically the genes describe acidification by the vacuolar ATPase, delivery of mannose-6-phosphate-tagged hydrolases such as glucocerebrosidase and hexosaminidase, ESCRT-dependent membrane scission, tethering and fusion by RAB7A, HOPS and adaptor complexes, and movement along microtubules via ARL8B and HOOK proteins. Ionic homeostasis through MCOLN1, TPCN2 and TMEM175 and transcriptional control by TFEB and TFE3 tune lysosome number and capacity to demand, linking the compartment to mTORC1 signalling and autophagy."
