    "description": "Protein folding is the chaperone system that gets newly made and stress-damaged polypeptides into their correct three-dimensional shapes and assembles them into complexes, the prerequisite for essentially every protein's activity. Without it cells accumulate misfolded and aggregated species, the outcome behind neurodegenerative proteinopathies, cataract, and secretory-cell stress. The genes span the major machines and their compartments: HSP70s with their DNAJ co-chaperones and nucleotide-exchange factors, HSP90 with CDC37 and the FKBP/PPI immunophilins, the cytosolic CCT/TRiC chamber with prefoldin for actin and tubulin, mitochondrial HSPD1/HSPE1, and small heat-shock proteins that hold unfolded clients soluble. In the endoplasmic reticulum, BiP, calnexin/calreticulin lectin cycles, UGGT1 quality control, and protein disulphide isomerases with ERO1 oxidases fold and proofread secretory and membrane proteins, linking this activity to the unfolded protein response, degradation triage, and collagen and antigen-presentation maturation."
