    "description": "cAMP/PKA signal transduction is the classic second-messenger cassette by which hormones and neuropeptides acting at G protein-coupled receptors change what a cell does within seconds and what genes it transcribes within hours. It underlies hormone responsiveness in endocrine tissue, cardiac contractility, memory-related synaptic plasticity, lipolysis and pigmentation, and its loss or constitutive activation produces endocrine tumours and syndromes such as Carney complex. Adenylate cyclase output raises cAMP, which binds regulatory subunits (PRKAR1A, PRKAR2B) to release catalytic PKA, while A-kinase anchoring proteins confine that activity to discrete subcellular microdomains and PKI restrains it. A large family of phosphodiesterases terminates the signal, giving each tissue its own kinetics and providing the drug targets exploited in inflammation, erectile dysfunction and heart failure. Downstream, CREB1, ATF1 and CREM with the coactivator CREBBP convert the transient signal into transcription. Upstream ligands here include VIP, CRH, GHRH, secretin and urocortin."
