    "name": "regulation of calcium-mediated signaling",
    "description": "Regulation of calcium-mediated signaling sets the gain on one of the most widely used intracellular messages: how strongly, how often, and for how long a cell responds when cytosolic calcium rises. Because calcium transients drive muscle contraction, immune activation, secretion, and activity-dependent gene expression in neurons, the regulators here determine whether those responses are appropriately scaled or become pathological, as in cardiac hypertrophy, excitotoxic neurodegeneration, and defective lymphocyte activation. Mechanistically the genes cover calcium sensors and buffers (calmodulins, CIB1, PCP4), release and clearance machinery (ITPR1, ATP2B4, sodium-calcium exchangers, P2X receptors), scaffolds that localise signalling to receptors and channels (AKAP5, HOMER2/3, FKBP1B), and the calcineurin holoenzyme with its inhibitor RCAN1, which converts sustained calcium into NFAT-dependent transcription. Antigen receptor components and the SYK/ZAP70 module tie the same logic to immune cell decisions."
