    "description": "regulation of phosphorylation covers the control layer above protein kinases and phosphatases: the ligands, receptors, adaptors, scaffolds, inhibitors and pseudokinases that set how much phosphate is added to a substrate and when. Because phosphorylation gates cell cycle entry, growth, survival, migration and immune activation, this regulation determines whether a cell divides or arrests, and its loss is a route into cancer and inflammatory disease. The genes span growth factor and cytokine inputs such as EGF, FGFs, VEGFA and the interleukins with their receptors, the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT-mTOR relays, CDK partners and CDK inhibitors like CDKN1A that time mitotic entry, and innate immune kinase modules built on MYD88, IRAK and TRAF adaptors. Counterweights are equally represented: PTEN and PTP-family phosphatases, DUSPs, SOCS proteins, tribbles pseudokinases and PKA regulatory subunits that restrain or redirect kinase output."
