    "description": "regulation of adenylate cyclase activity governs how much cyclic AMP a cell makes, and therefore how strongly it responds to hormones and neurotransmitters that work through this second messenger. cAMP output sets heart rate and contractility, water reabsorption in the kidney, insulin release from beta cells, and the strength of synaptic signalling, so its tuning is the point at which many drugs act. The genes describe both directions of control: stimulatory receptors for peptide hormones and neuropeptides such as the vasopressin, GLP-1, GIP and CRH receptors acting through Gs, opposed by adenosine, chemokine, GABA-B, metabotropic glutamate and galanin receptors coupling through inhibitory Gi subunits. Additional layers come from calcium, supplied by voltage-gated channels and by STIM1-ORAI1 store-operated entry, from cyclase-associated proteins, and from AKAP scaffolds that place the enzyme next to its targets."
