    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Gamma-carboxylation of protein precursors",
    "description": "Gamma-carboxylation of protein precursors is the vitamin K-dependent modification that makes coagulation factors able to bind calcium and dock onto membrane surfaces, and it is the step targeted by warfarin. Without it, prothrombin, factors VII, IX and X, protein C, protein S and protein Z circulate but cannot assemble on phospholipid at a site of injury, producing bleeding disorders; loss of the carboxylase itself causes a combined deficiency of all these factors. In the endoplasmic reticulum, a single enzyme recognises a propeptide motif on the amino-terminal side of a glutamate cluster and processively converts each glutamate to gamma-carboxyglutamate before releasing the substrate. The same modification serves biology beyond clotting: osteocalcin in bone mineralisation and Gas6 in growth factor-like signalling depend on it, linking vitamin K status to haemostasis, skeletal and vascular function."
