    "description": "G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathway, coupled to cyclic nucleotide second messenger is the route by which hormones, neurotransmitters, chemokines and drugs change the intracellular concentration of cAMP or cGMP, converting an extracellular ligand into a graded, amplified change in kinase activity and gene expression. It sets heart rate, smooth muscle tone, steroid and thyroid hormone output, lipolysis, pigmentation, mood and nociception, and it is the target of a large share of prescribed medicines. Receptors here couple through Gs to stimulate adenylate cyclase or through Gi/Go to inhibit it, with cAMP acting mainly on protein kinase A and Epac. The membership spans adrenergic, muscarinic, serotonergic, histaminergic, opioid, cannabinoid, somatostatin, melanocortin and glycoprotein hormone receptors, so receptor pairs on the same cell can push cyclase in opposite directions and tune the response. Loss or constitutive activation of individual receptors causes endocrine and metabolic disease."
