    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Mitochondrial biogenesis",
    "description": "Mitochondrial biogenesis is how a cell builds new mitochondria and matches its respiratory capacity to demand, the adaptation that lets exercised muscle gain endurance and that fails in metabolic disease, heart failure and ageing. Calcium influx and energy depletion are the initiating signals: AMPK senses a falling ATP:AMP ratio, p38 MAPK and CaMKIV respond to contraction and calcium, and both converge on the coactivator PGC-1alpha, with PGC-1beta and PPRC acting similarly. PGC-1alpha binds no DNA itself but partners with NRF1, GABP, ESRRA and nuclear receptors such as PPARA/RXRA to drive nuclear genes for oxidative phosphorylation subunits, the MICOS cristae-shaping complex, protein import machinery and antioxidant enzymes. It also switches on TFAM, TFB2M and POLRMT, coupling nuclear transcription to replication and expression of the mitochondrial genome, while sirtuins and corepressors such as NCOR1 tune the response."
