    "name": "regulation of mitochondrial DNA metabolic process",
    "description": "regulation of mitochondrial DNA metabolic process controls how the mitochondrial genome is copied, repaired and turned over, setting the copy number and integrity of the DNA that encodes core respiratory chain subunits. Because each cell carries many mitochondrial genomes, this regulation determines whether damaged or mutant molecules accumulate to the threshold at which oxidative phosphorylation fails, as happens in mitochondrial myopathies, depletion syndromes and age-related decline in tissue energetics. The activities represented span nicking and degradation of mitochondrial DNA by an endonuclease, sealing of nicks during base excision repair by DNA ligase III, stabilisation of the single-stranded template at the replication fork by mitochondrial single-stranded DNA binding protein, and adenine methylation that marks the genome. Together these steps couple mitochondrial genome maintenance to nuclear signals, cell death execution and metabolic demand."
