    "description": "Regulation of MITF-M-dependent genes involved in DNA replication, damage repair and senescence describes how the melanocyte master transcription factor MITF, beyond pigmentation, sustains the machinery that copies and protects the genome. This link explains why melanocytes and melanoma cells tolerate proliferation without accumulating lethal damage, and why lowering MITF levels drives replication stress, DNA damage and entry into senescence rather than simple growth arrest. MITF-M binds promoters of replication and repair genes including the MCM helicase subunits MCM2 and MCM5, the ligase LIG1, the homologous recombination factor BRCA1, and the telomerase catalytic subunit TERT, coupling lineage identity to S-phase competence and chromosome stability. The outcome of MITF loss depends on context: depletion accompanied by ATF4-driven translational reprogramming yields reversible arrest instead of senescence, a switch relevant to melanoma phenotype-switching and drug tolerance."
