    "source": "go",
    "name": "protein methylation",
    "description": "Protein methylation covers the transfer of methyl groups from S-adenosylmethionine onto amino acid side chains and protein termini, a modification that tunes protein interactions, stability and activity without changing sequence. It sets the histone marks that define transcriptional states — H3K9 by EHMT1/EHMT2, H3K36 by SETD2, H4K20 by KMT5A, H3K4 by SETD7 — and equally modifies non-histone substrates: arginine methylation by the PRMT family regulates Sm proteins and spliceosome assembly, actin histidine methylation by SETD3 shapes cytoskeletal function, and dedicated methyltransferases mark translation factors eEF1A, eEF2 and the release factor, chaperones, ATP synthase and respiratory complex I subunits. Related activities methylate protein N-termini, prenylated C-termini for membrane anchoring, and repair isoaspartyl damage. Loss of individual enzymes causes developmental syndromes, congenital insensitivity to pain, myopathy and cancer, reflecting how broadly gene expression, RNA processing and mitochondrial assembly depend on this chemistry."
