    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "RHOA GTPase cycle",
    "description": "RHOA GTPase cycle describes how the founding member of the RHO GTPase family is switched between GDP- and GTP-bound states, and what that switch controls: the actomyosin cytoskeleton and cell contractility. Through it cells adhere, migrate, divide, traffic vesicles, sense mechanical force and adjust gene expression, making it the hub of mechanotransduction in endothelium and smooth muscle, of neurite outgrowth and axon retraction, and of immune cell motility. Exchange factors such as ARHGEF1, ARHGEF12 and TRIO load GTP; a large family of ARHGAP proteins, DLC1 and myosin-IX GAPs terminate signalling; ARHGDIA sequesters the inactive pool. Active RHOA engages ROCK1/2, citron kinase, PKN kinases, formins and rhotekin to drive stress fibre assembly and cytokinetic furrow ingression. RHOA is recurrently mutated in lymphoma and gastric cancer, and bacterial toxins hijack it to subvert the host cytoskeleton."
