    "name": "Mitotic G1 phase and G1/S transition",
    "description": "Mitotic G1 phase and G1/S transition covers the interval in which a cell decides whether to divide, and the commitment step that makes that decision irreversible once DNA replication begins. It sets the restriction point that mitogens act on, and its loss is among the most common events in cancer. In quiescent and early G1 cells the pocket proteins p130 and p107 with E2F4/5 and the MuvB core assemble repressive DREAM complexes that silence cell cycle genes, while RB1 restrains E2F1-3. Mitogen-driven D-type cyclins with CDK4/6, then cyclin E-CDK2, phosphorylate RB1 and release E2F-dependent transcription of replication factors -- MCM helicase subunits, ORC and CDC6/CDT1 licensing proteins, polymerases, PCNA and nucleotide-synthesis enzymes. INK4 and Cip/Kip inhibitors oppose these kinases, and SCF-proteasome degradation of p27 and CDT1 enforces the switch and prevents re-replication."
