    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of intrinsic apoptotic signaling pathway in response to DNA damage by p53 class mediator",
    "description": "regulation of intrinsic apoptotic signaling pathway in response to DNA damage by p53 class mediator sets the threshold at which a cell with damaged DNA decides to kill itself rather than repair and continue. This decision determines whether genotoxic stress ends in tumour suppression or in the survival of a mutated clone, and its tuning explains much of why cancer cells resist chemotherapy and radiation. The genes act on p53 itself and on the mitochondrial step it commands: acetylation-dependent control through SIRT1 and KDM1A, cofactor and coactivator input via TAF9 and ZNF385A, translational amplification of p53 by RPL26 and HNRNPK, and direct restraint of mitochondrial permeabilisation by BCL2 and TRIAP1. Others, including MIF, CD74, MUC1 and CD44, dampen the response in a way that favours survival. The pathway therefore sits where DNA damage checkpoints hand over to the mitochondrial apoptotic machinery."
