    "source": "go",
    "name": "apoptotic signaling pathway",
    "description": "apoptotic signaling pathway covers the routes by which a cell receives a death cue and commits to dismantling itself, from ligand binding or internal damage sensing up to the point where executioner caspases are unleashed. It is how tissues delete surplus, infected, damaged or mutated cells, and its failure permits tumour survival and autoimmunity while its overactivation drives neurodegeneration and ischaemic injury. Both principal arms are represented: death receptors such as FAS, TNFRSF1A and TRAIL receptors recruiting FADD, TRADD and caspase-8, and the mitochondrial arm in which BH3-only proteins including BID, BAD, PMAIP1 and BBC3 overcome BCL2 and MCL1 to let BAX and BAK release cytochrome c, activating APAF1 and caspase-9. Layered onto this are p53-driven damage responses, ER stress signalling through PERK, ATF4 and CHOP, kinase and ubiquitin-ligase modulators, IAP inhibition by DIABLO, and microRNAs that set the threshold for death."
