    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Intrinsic Pathway for Apoptosis",
    "description": "Intrinsic Pathway for Apoptosis is the mitochondrial route to programmed cell death, used when a cell judges its own internal state to be beyond repair — DNA damage, growth factor withdrawal, endoplasmic reticulum stress, cytoskeletal or oncogenic insult. It removes damaged and potentially transformed cells, and its suppression is one of the ways tumours become immortal and resistant to chemotherapy. BH3-only sensors such as BIM, BID, PUMA and NOXA are induced or released and neutralise BCL-2 and BCL-XL, freeing BAX and BAK to permeabilise the mitochondrial outer membrane. Cytochrome c release nucleates the APAF1 apoptosome, activating caspase-9 and then the executioner caspases-3 and -7, while SMAC/DIABLO relieves XIAP inhibition. Upstream, p53 family transcription factors and 14-3-3 and AKT survival signalling set the threshold, and caspase cleavage of gasdermins can divert the outcome toward lytic death."
