   "blurb": "17 pathways from go 12, reactome 5, covering 553 distinct genes. The label above is provisional: it is the three most distinctive terms in the member names, not a written theme name. No theme has been named or described.",
   "exemplar": {
    "key": "go:GO:1905335",
    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of aggrephagy",
    "description": "Regulation of aggrephagy controls how aggressively a cell routes misfolded, aggregated protein into autophagosomes for lysosomal destruction. This is the principal defence against toxic inclusions in long-lived cells such as neurones and muscle fibres, and its weakening underlies protein-aggregation diseases including Huntington disease and myofibrillar myopathy. The core machinery here is the chaperone-assisted selective autophagy module, in which HSPB8 and the co-chaperone BAG3 recognise unfolded client protein and hand it toward the microtubule motor adaptor HDAC6, which transports cargo along tubulin tracks to the perinuclear aggresome for engulfment. Casein kinase 2 phosphorylates the sequestosome-type receptors to strengthen ubiquitin binding, while acetylation by KAT5 and cycles of S-acylation and depalmitoylation tune adaptor localisation. Huntingtin itself acts as a scaffold in this selective autophagy route, linking cargo recognition to autophagosome formation."
   },
