    "source": "go",
    "name": "mitophagy",
    "description": "Mitophagy is the quality-control route by which whole mitochondria are captured in autophagosomes and delivered to lysosomes, letting cells cull organelles that are depolarised, damaged, or simply surplus. It sets mitochondrial number during reticulocyte maturation and after metabolic remodelling, and its failure allows damaged organelles to accumulate and leak reactive oxygen species and mitochondrial DNA, a defect central to autosomal recessive Parkinson disease. The best-defined arm is ubiquitin-driven: the kinase PINK1 accumulates on depolarised outer membranes, activates the ligase Parkin, and the resulting ubiquitin chains recruit OPTN and SQSTM1, with USP30 opposing the mark. Receptor-mediated arms act independently, through BNIP3, BNIP3L, FUNDC1, BCL2L13 and inner-membrane prohibitin, binding LC3 and GABARAP directly. Core autophagy machinery, ULK1 initiation, ATG conjugation, and fission by DNM1L then execute engulfment, tying this pathway to hypoxia, iron handling and metabolic reprogramming."
