    "description": "Heme A biosynthetic process produces the specialized heme variant that sits in the catalytic core of cytochrome c oxidase, the terminal enzyme of the respiratory chain. Without it, complex IV cannot assemble or transfer electrons to oxygen, and cells lose oxidative phosphorylation capacity — the basis of infantile cardiomyopathy, Leigh syndrome and other mitochondrial encephalomyopathies caused by mutations in the farnesyltransferase COX10 and the monooxygenase COX15. The route begins with the shared tetrapyrrole pathway, in which succinyl-CoA and glycine are condensed by ALAS1 and successive enzymes build the porphyrin ring, ending with ferrochelatase inserting iron to give heme B. Heme B is then modified inside mitochondria: COX10 farnesylates a vinyl group to yield heme O, and COX15 hydroxylates a methyl group to give heme A. The pathway thus links iron and porphyrin metabolism directly to respiratory chain biogenesis."
