    "source": "go",
    "name": "detoxification of copper ion",
    "description": "Detoxification of copper ion protects cells from the redox chemistry of free copper, which catalyses hydroxyl radical formation and displaces metals from the active sites of other proteins. Cells need copper as an enzyme cofactor but can tolerate almost none of it unbound, so excess is buffered or exported. The bulk of this capacity comes from the metallothionein family, small cysteine-rich proteins induced by metal load that clamp copper in thiolate clusters and hold it in an inert pool; ATP7A moves copper out of the cytosol into the secretory pathway for efflux or delivery to cuproenzymes, and DJ-1 contributes antioxidant defence against copper-driven damage. Loss of this buffering underlies copper toxicity in liver and brain, links to Wilson and Menkes disease physiology, and modulates sensitivity to copper-dependent cell death; the same metallothioneins also handle zinc and cadmium."
