    "source": "go",
    "name": "peptidyl-lysine acetylation",
    "description": "Peptidyl-lysine acetylation is the transfer of acetyl groups onto lysine side chains of proteins, a reversible modification that neutralises the residue's positive charge and creates docking sites for bromodomain readers. It is the chemistry behind histone acetylation and therefore behind open chromatin and transcriptional activation, but it extends far beyond the nucleus: acetylation of tubulin by ATAT1 marks stable microtubules, and acetylation of metabolic enzymes and transcription factors tunes their activity to acetyl-CoA availability. The genes span the major acetyltransferase families, from the CREBBP/EP300 coactivators that couple signal-responsive transcription factors to the general machinery, through GCN5/PCAF and the MYST enzymes KAT5 and KAT7 that act in DNA damage response and replication licensing, to ESCO1, which acetylates cohesin to establish sister chromatid cohesion. SIRT1 provides the opposing deacetylase activity, making the mark a dynamic, nutrient-sensitive switch."
