    "description": "ferroptosis is an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death driven by runaway peroxidation of membrane phospholipids rather than by caspases, and it is the vulnerability exploited to kill therapy-resistant and mesenchymal tumour cells, as well as a contributor to neurodegeneration, ischaemia-reperfusion injury and kidney damage. The balance is set by cystine supply through SLC7A11, glutathione, and GPX4, which reduces lipid hydroperoxides; parallel defence comes from FSP1 (AIFM2) and NQO1 acting on ubiquinone, and from NRF2-driven antioxidant and iron-handling genes including HMOX1 and ferritin. On the pro-death side, ACSL4 loads polyunsaturated fatty acids into membrane lipids, making them oxidisable, while labile iron and cell-cycle- and stress-linked kinases push peroxidation forward. Serine and citrate metabolism, autophagic ferritin turnover via SQSTM1, and NINJ1-dependent membrane rupture link the process to nutrient state and to inflammatory release of cell contents."
