    "name": "polyamine biosynthetic process",
    "description": "polyamine biosynthetic process builds putrescine, spermidine and spermine, the small polycations that cells require for growth, ribosome function and chromatin compaction. Proliferating tissues, spermatogenesis and wound repair depend on it, and its deregulation is a hallmark of tumours, which is why ornithine decarboxylase inhibition is pursued therapeutically. Ornithine decarboxylase commits ornithine to putrescine, while adenosylmethionine decarboxylase supplies the aminopropyl donor used by spermidine and spermine synthases. Flux is held under unusually tight control: antizymes target ornithine decarboxylase for proteasomal destruction without ubiquitin, antizyme inhibitors relieve that block, and acetylation by SAT1 followed by oxidation by PAOX or SMOX either exports or recycles the higher polyamines in a back-conversion loop. An agmatinase route offers an alternative entry from arginine. The pathway ties amino acid and methionine metabolism to translational control, including polyamine-dependent hypusination and eIF5A activity."
