    "source": "go",
    "name": "behavior",
    "description": "behavior covers the nervous-system-dependent actions an animal takes in response to internal states and external cues tion — feeding, sleep, mating, locomotion, learning, fear and social interaction — and the molecules that generate them. It matters because these outputs define an animal's fitness, and their disruption produces autism, addiction, obesity, anxiety and movement disorders. The genes span the layers that build such responses: neurotransmitter systems (dopamine, serotonin, glutamate, GABA, opioid and cholinergic receptors and their transporters), synaptic adhesion and scaffolding proteins such as neurexins, neuroligins and SHANK family members that specify circuit wiring, activity-dependent immediate-early transcription (FOS, EGR1, ARC, NPAS4) that converts firing into lasting change, and hypothalamic peptide signalling — leptin, melanocortin, orexin, oxytocin, NPY — that sets appetite, arousal and social drive. Circadian regulators and ion channels controlling excitability tie these outputs to timing and state."
