    "description": "cellular response to L-glutamate covers what a cell does once glutamate, the main excitatory neurotransmitter of the vertebrate brain, engages its receptors: change synaptic strength, remodel the postsynaptic actin cytoskeleton, alter gene expression, and in excess die by excitotoxicity. It underlies learning and memory at glutamatergic synapses and is the point of failure in ischaemic neuronal death and in neurodegenerative disease. The genes span receptor and transducer: AMPA and NMDA receptor subunits such as GRIA1 and GRIN2D admit calcium and sodium, the tyrosine kinase FYN phosphorylates NMDA receptors to tune their gating, the calcium sensor hippocalcin couples the resulting calcium rise to receptor internalisation during long-term depression, and BAIAP2 links activity to dendritic spine morphology. Parkin ties glutamate signalling to synaptic protein turnover and mitochondrial quality control in neurons under excitatory load."
