    "source": "go",
    "name": "response to glucagon",
    "description": "response to glucagon is the set of cellular changes triggered when the pancreatic alpha-cell hormone glucagon signals that blood glucose is falling. It is the counter-regulatory arm of glucose homeostasis: in hepatocytes it switches metabolism from storage to output, driving glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ureagenesis and ketogenesis, and its loss or excess underlies fasting hypoglycaemia and the inappropriate hepatic glucose production of diabetes. Glucagon and the related incretin peptides act through class B G protein-coupled receptors (GCGR, GLP1R, GLP2R) coupled via Gs to adenylate cyclases; the resulting cAMP releases catalytic PKA from its regulatory subunits, which phosphorylates metabolic enzymes and activates CREB1 to reprogram transcription. Downstream targets seen here include urea cycle enzymes CPS1 and ASS1, the ketogenic enzyme HMGCS2, and lipogenic control through SREBF1, linking hormone sensing to fuel selection, amino acid disposal and circadian metabolic timing."
