    "description": "response to cocaine covers the cellular and behavioural changes triggered when cocaine blocks monoamine reuptake, the biology that underlies psychostimulant reward, sensitisation and addiction. Cocaine's immediate targets are here as the dopamine and serotonin transporters, and the consequences run through dopamine receptors D1-D5 and the striatal signalling hub DARPP-32, with CDK5, FOSB and CREB1 converting acute stimulation into lasting transcriptional change. Chromatin and DNA methylation enzymes such as HDAC5, EHMT2 and DNMT3A make that change persistent, which is why drug-associated plasticity outlives the drug. Glutamatergic components including GRIA1, HOMER1/2 and the astrocytic glutamate transporters remodel synapses in reward circuitry, while CRH, oxytocin, prodynorphin-related and kappa-opioid signalling link drug exposure to stress and aversive withdrawal states. COMT and MAO-adjacent metabolic genes set individual sensitivity, tying this pathway to relapse risk and to dopaminergic neuron identity."
