    "description": "cellular response to thyroid hormone stimulus covers what a cell does once triiodothyronine reaches it: alter gene expression, enzyme output and secretion in ways that set metabolic rate, growth and differentiation. This response underlies development of the brain, bone and cochlea and the regulation of energy expenditure; its loss produces resistance to thyroid hormone, with goitre, deafness and impaired growth. The core is nuclear receptor signalling through thyroid hormone receptor beta, which switches from repression to activation on ligand binding and recruits coactivator complex subunits such as MED1 and BRD8 to target promoters. Downstream effects here include thyroglobulin processing and hormone liberation by lysosomal cathepsins, glutathione synthesis via the glutamate-cysteine ligase subunits, and endocrine crosstalk through growth hormone secretagogue receptor and inhibin beta B, linking thyroid status to redox balance and other hormonal axes."
