    "description": "Nucleotide metabolic process covers the synthesis, salvage, interconversion and breakdown of purine and pyrimidine nucleotides, the molecules that supply DNA and RNA precursors, carry cellular energy as ATP, and act as second messengers. Its output sets the pace of proliferation, and its failures cause hyperuricaemia and gout, immunodeficiency from adenosine deaminase or purine nucleoside phosphorylase loss, and mitochondrial DNA depletion when deoxyribonucleotide pools are unbalanced. The genes span de novo synthesis from phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate (PRPS1, PPAT, CAD, UMPS), salvage kinases and phosphorylases (HPRT1, APRT, DCK, TK2), ribonucleotide reduction, and the nucleotidases and Nudix hydrolases that sanitise damaged nucleotides. Tightly coupled to it are glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway supplying ribose and reducing power, oxidative phosphorylation regenerating ATP, NAD biosynthesis, and cyclases and phosphodiesterases governing cyclic AMP and GMP signalling, with AMPK and mTOR reading nucleotide status."
