    "source": "go",
    "name": "pyruvate metabolic process",
    "description": "Pyruvate metabolic process covers the production and disposal of pyruvate, the hub metabolite where glycolysis meets mitochondrial oxidation, lactate production and gluconeogenesis. It determines whether a cell burns glucose aerobically or ferments it, and so underlies muscle performance, hepatic glucose output, the Warburg-like shift of proliferating and hypoxic cells, and the lactic acidosis and encephalopathy that follow enzyme deficiency. The genes span the full glycolytic chain from hexokinase and phosphofructokinase through GAPDH, enolase and pyruvate kinase, the lactate dehydrogenases and monocarboxylate transporters that export or import lactate, the mitochondrial carrier and pyruvate dehydrogenase complex with its PDK kinases and PDP phosphatases, malic enzymes, and the PCK1/FBP1 gluconeogenic exits. Layered on top is regulation: HIF1A and MYC-like transcriptional control, insulin and IGF1 input, AMPK and mTOR nutrient sensing, and sirtuin and acetylation-dependent tuning of flux."
