    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Inositol phosphate metabolism",
    "description": "Inositol phosphate metabolism generates and interconverts the soluble inositol phosphates that carry intracellular signals, beginning with the inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate released when phospholipase C cleaves PI(4,5)P2 to trigger calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum. Beyond that classical second messenger, successive kinase and phosphatase steps build a large family of higher species — IP4, IP5, IP6 and the energetic pyrophosphates IP7 and IP8 — which tune insulin secretion, phosphate and iron sensing, vesicle traffic, chromatin regulation and mRNA export. The genes span the phospholipase C isozymes, the multikinases IPMK, IPPK and IP6K1-3, diphosphoinositol pyrophosphate kinases and hydrolases, and the 5-phosphatases and PTEN-family enzymes that reverse the pathway; inositol synthase and monophosphatases recycle free myo-inositol, the latter being the target of lithium. Because synthesis shuttles between cytosol and nucleus, nucleoporins appear as the transport route. Loss of individual enzymes causes Lowe syndrome, and disrupted signalling in cancer and neurological disease."
