    "description": "mammary gland epithelial cell proliferation is the controlled expansion of the epithelium that lines mammary ducts and alveoli, the process that builds the ductal tree at puberty, remodels it during pregnancy and lactation, and, when deregulated, produces breast cancer. It is largely a hormone-driven program: estrogen acting through ESR1 with the MED1 coactivator, and progesterone acting indirectly by inducing amphiregulin and RANKL (TNFSF11) to signal to neighbouring cells, converge on cyclin D1 and ERK to license cell cycle entry. Lineage transcription factors such as GATA3, CEBPB and TFAP2C specify luminal identity while proliferation proceeds, and HOXA5, ID2 and KDM5B restrain or time it. Guidance and morphogenetic cues including WNT5A, ROBO1 and EPHA2 couple division to branching architecture, and BRCA2 and BAX link the proliferative state to genome maintenance and apoptotic culling of damaged cells."
