    "description": "Androgen biosynthetic process builds testosterone and dihydrotestosterone from steroid precursors, the hormones that drive masculinisation of the fetal genitalia, pubertal virilisation, spermatogenesis and the growth of prostate and hair follicles. It operates mainly in testicular Leydig cells and the adrenal cortex, with peripheral tissues finishing the job: CYP17A1 performs the hydroxylase and lyase steps that divert pregnenolone away from cortisol toward C19 steroids, 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenases and HSD17B3 complete testosterone synthesis, and SRD5A2 reduces it to the far more potent dihydrogenase-resistant dihydrotestosterone in target tissues. Nuclear receptor coactivation by MED1 and gonadal patterning input from WNT4 link hormone output to the transcriptional programme of the steroidogenic cell. Loss of individual enzymes causes disorders of sex development, 46,XY undervirilisation, or congenital adrenal hyperplasia, while excess flux underlies hyperandrogenism and fuels prostate cancer growth."
