    "source": "go",
    "name": "response to salt stress",
    "description": "Response to salt stress is the set of adjustments a cell or organism makes when external salt concentration rises or falls, protecting cells from osmotic swelling, shrinkage and ionic damage. In humans this matters most in the kidney medulla, where cells routinely face extreme sodium chloride and urea gradients, and in systemic control of water balance: vasopressin release, aquaporin-mediated water permeability, and sodium transport by Na/K-ATPase regulatory subunits and cation-chloride cotransporters together set urine concentration and blood volume. Downstream responses include accumulation of compatible osmolytes through aldose reductase, mechanosensitive calcium entry via TRPV4, chaperone induction, mitochondrial calcium handling, and mRNA destabilisation programmes that reshape gene expression. When the stress exceeds adaptive capacity, apoptotic and inflammatory routes engage, linking hypertonicity to tubular cell death, cytokine release and impaired concentrating ability."
