    "description": "regulation of systemic arterial blood pressure mediated by a chemical signal is the hormonal and locally released chemistry that sets vascular tone and blood volume, keeping perfusion pressure within limits from beat to beat and across days. Its failure produces hypertension, and with it heart failure, stroke and renal damage; several of its members are the targets of the most widely used antihypertensive drugs. The core is the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, in which renin and angiotensin-converting enzyme generate angiotensin II acting through AGTR1, while ACE2 and MAS1 form an opposing degradative arm and aldosterone synthase couples the system to sodium handling. Around it sit vasoconstrictors and vasodilators acting in parallel -- endothelins and their receptors, vasopressin, adrenergic and thrombin receptors, tachykinins, and endothelial nitric oxide synthase -- together with mast cell proteases and peptidases that process the peptide ligands, and redox enzymes that modulate endothelial responsiveness."
