    "description": "Intracellular water homeostasis keeps cell volume and osmolarity within tolerable limits, allowing cells to survive osmotic challenge without swelling, shrinking, or lysing. It matters wherever fluid moves across epithelia and membranes in bulk: kidney tubules concentrating urine, brain astrocytes buffering the perivascular space, and secretory tissues handling large water fluxes. The core machinery is the aquaporin water channels, which provide selective transmembrane pores rather than relying on slow diffusion through the lipid bilayer; AQP1 serves vascular and tubular water flow, AQP4 dominates astrocyte endfeet and shapes brain water content and oedema, and AQP11 maintains intracellular and endoplasmic reticulum compartments, with its loss producing renal cyst formation. Hormonal input from secretin and its receptor links water transport to ductal fluid secretion, while CLN3 ties lysosomal and vacuolar volume control into the same regulatory picture."
