    "name": "lens fiber cell differentiation",
    "description": "Lens fiber cell differentiation builds the transparent bulk of the ocular lens, converting proliferating anterior epithelial cells into elongated, organelle-free fibers packed with crystallins. Transparency and refractive power depend on it, and disruption of these genes produces congenital cataract, microphthalmia and lens degeneration. Cells first exit the cell cycle under CDKN1B and CDKN1C control, then execute a transcriptional program driven by MAF, PROX1, PITX3, FOXE3, SIX3 and HSF4 that switches on alpha- and gamma-crystallins. Elongation requires cytoskeletal remodelling through the beaded filament proteins BFSP1 and BFSP2, vimentin and tropomodulin, with cell-cell adhesion and membrane organization maintained by NF2 and ephrin-EPHA2 signalling. FGF, TGF-beta and WNT inputs pattern the differentiation gradient across the lens and are tuned by SPRY and SPRED feedback inhibitors, while programmed organelle and nuclear breakdown clears light-scattering structures from mature fibers."
