    "source": "go",
    "name": "cellular response to oxygen levels",
    "description": "cellular response to oxygen levels is how cells sense oxygen availability and rewire their metabolism, growth and survival to match it. It underlies adaptation to hypoxia in ischaemia, wound healing and tumour growth, and its constitutive activation through VHL loss drives clear cell renal carcinoma and polycythaemia. The core is a hydroxylation-based oxygen sensor: prolyl hydroxylases (EGLN1-3) and VHL mark HIF1A and EPAS1 for degradation when oxygen is plentiful, so that under hypoxia the HIF-ARNT heterodimer accumulates and induces glycolytic enzymes, PDK1, VEGFA and erythropoietic and angiogenic factors. Downstream branches shift ATP production away from oxidative phosphorylation, trigger BNIP3- and PINK1-dependent mitophagy and autophagy, restrain mTOR signalling and the cell cycle, and, if stress persists, engage p53-dependent apoptosis. Additional layers include microRNAs such as miR-210, sirtuins, oxidative-stress responses and pH regulation through vacuolar ATPases and NHE1."
