   "exemplar": {
    "key": "reactome:R-HSA-9860931",
    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Response of endothelial cells to shear stress",
    "description": "Response of endothelial cells to shear stress describes how the cells lining blood vessels sense the frictional force of flowing blood and translate it into vessel tone, cell shape, and inflammatory state. It explains why arterial regions of steady high flow stay atheroprotected while branch points and curvatures with disturbed or sluggish flow become sites of atherosclerotic plaque. All flow types open the mechanosensitive channel PIEZO1, admitting calcium that activates calpain-2 and remodels the submembrane cytoskeleton so cells elongate along the flow axis. Steady laminar flow additionally engages VEGFR2, purinergic P2RY2 and adrenomedullin signalling through PI3K-AKT to phosphorylate eNOS, raising nitric oxide and driving vasodilation. Disturbed flow instead works through integrin alpha5-beta1, PDE4D-mediated cAMP loss, PP2A-dependent YAP1 activation and FAK-driven NF-kB nuclear entry, switching on pro-inflammatory transcription and adhesion molecule expression."
   },
