    "source": "go",
    "name": "cellular response to stress",
    "description": "cellular response to stress is the broad umbrella covering how cells detect damaging conditions — DNA lesions, misfolded proteins, oxidants, hypoxia, nutrient shortage, heat, inflammatory and mechanical insults — and reprogram themselves to survive or die. It determines whether an injured cell repairs itself, arrests, senesces, or is eliminated, so it underlies tumour suppression, ageing, neurodegeneration, and the response to radiation and chemotherapy. The genes span the major arms: ATM/ATR–CHEK–TP53 damage signalling with excision, mismatch, Fanconi and double-strand break repair machinery; the unfolded protein response through ERN1, ATF6 and XBP1 with ER-associated degradation and chaperones; heat shock factors and HSP70/HSP90 proteostasis; NFE2L2–KEAP1 and peroxidase/superoxide dismutase antioxidant defence; HIF1A-driven hypoxia adaptation; mTOR and AMPK nutrient sensing with autophagy and mitophagy; and stress-activated MAPK, integrated stress response translational control, and BCL2-family apoptotic execution."
