    "name": "sensory perception",
    "description": "Sensory perception covers how the nervous system detects stimuli from the outside world and converts them into neural signals the brain can interpret. It defines the specialized receptor cells of the eye, ear, nose, tongue and skin, and its failure produces the common sensory disorders: retinitis pigmentosa and macular degeneration, congenital and progressive deafness, anosmia, cataract, and inherited insensitivity or hypersensitivity to pain. Mechanistically it spans distinct transduction logics united by a common plan of receptor, ion channel and amplifying cascade -- rhodopsin and cone opsins feeding transducin, phosphodiesterase and cyclic-nucleotide-gated channels in photoreceptors; a very large odorant receptor repertoire with taste and vomeronasal receptors acting through gustducin and phospholipase C; hair-cell mechanotransduction built from cadherin-23, protocadherin-15, myosins and TMC channels; and nociceptor TRP, ASIC, PIEZO and voltage-gated sodium channels. Developmental regulators such as PAX6, CRX and POU4F3 specify these cells, while ciliary trafficking and retinoid recycling maintain them."
