   "exemplar": {
    "key": "reactome:R-HSA-9717189",
    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Sensory perception of taste",
    "description": "Sensory perception of taste is how taste bud cells on the tongue detect sweet, umami, bitter, sour and salty chemicals and convert them into signals the gustatory nerves can read, guiding food selection, avoidance of toxins and the regulation of appetite. Sweet and umami are read by TAS1R heterodimers and bitter by the large TAS2R family, all coupling through gustducin and PLCB2 to IP3-mediated calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum; calcium opens TRPM5 and TRPM4, and voltage-gated sodium channels amplify the resulting depolarization until CALHM1:CALHM3 channels release ATP as the transmitter onto afferent fibres. Sour taste instead depends on proton entry through OTOP1 and block of KCNJ2 in a separate cell type, while low salt is sensed by epithelial sodium channel subunits. Variation in bitter receptors such as TAS2R38 underlies well-known individual differences in taste sensitivity."
   },
