   "exemplar": {
    "key": "go:GO:0002286",
    "source": "go",
    "name": "T cell activation involved in immune response",
    "description": "T cell activation involved in immune response describes how a resting T cell, on encountering peptide-MHC together with costimulation and cytokines, changes shape, proliferates, and acquires an effector programme that drives host defence. It sets the tone of adaptive immunity: too little gives susceptibility to intracellular pathogens, too much gives autoimmunity and inflammatory disease. The genes span the recognition step -- class II MHC and its chaperone, CD80/CD86 and ICOS ligands, adhesion via ICAM1 and LFA-1 -- and the signalling that follows through PI3K-mTOR, PKC-MALT1-NF-κB, and JAK1/JAK3-STAT modules. Cytokine inputs including IL-2, IL-6, IL-12, IL-18, IL-23, IL-27 and the interferons feed master transcription factors such as TBX21, GATA3, RORC, EOMES, BCL6 and FOXP3 that fix helper and regulatory lineage identity. Proteasome subunits, SOCS proteins, ZC3H12A and the roquin ligases restrain the response, and antigen processing supplies it."
   },
