    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of cell activation",
    "description": "regulation of cell activation governs how strongly and how long a cell responds after it meets an activating ligand, and in practice this is largely the control of immune cell activation u2014 lymphocyte, myeloid, mast cell and platelet responses that must be switched on to clear infection and switched off to avoid autoimmunity and chronic inflammation. The genes cover both directions of that balance: antigen receptor proximal kinases and adaptors such as LCK, ZAP70, SYK, BTK, LAT and VAV1; costimulatory receptor u2013ligand pairs including CD28, CD40/CD40LG, ICOS and the TNF superfamily; inhibitory checkpoints such as CTLA4, PDCD1, LAG3, TIGIT and BTLA; phosphatases and ubiquitin ligases (PTPN22, PTPN6, INPP5D, CBLB, TNFAIP3) that raise the activation threshold; MHC class II and antigen presentation components; and cytokines with their JAK u2013STAT outputs that set lineage bias. Transcriptional and chromatin regulators, including FOXP3, TBX21, GATA3 and SWI/SNF subunits, fix the resulting activation state."
