    "source": "go",
    "name": "positive regulation of alpha-beta T cell proliferation",
    "description": "positive regulation of alpha-beta T cell proliferation covers the signals that drive conventional T cells to expand clonally after they recognise antigen, converting a handful of specific cells into the effector population that clears an infection. Without it immunity is blunted; unchecked, it feeds autoimmunity and chronic inflammation. Three input layers are represented: the T cell receptor complex itself with its proximal kinases and phosphatase (CD3E, ZAP70, SYK, PTPRC) reading peptide presented on MHC class I; costimulation through CD28 and OX40 ligand that licenses full activation rather than anergy; and cytokines of the IL-12 family, including IL-12, IL-23 and IL-27 subunits with their receptors, plus IL-18, which sustain division and bias differentiation toward Th1 and Th17 fates. Chemokine and adhesion components position cells for these encounters, while TGF-beta receptor signalling sets the counterweight."
