    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Nef Mediated CD4 Down-regulation",
    "description": "Nef Mediated CD4 Down-regulation describes how the HIV-1 accessory protein Nef strips its own entry receptor from the surface of an infected cell. Removing CD4 prevents newly made Env from being captured by receptor on the producer cell, so virions are released efficiently and remain infectious, and it simultaneously blunts helper T cell signalling and superinfection. CD4 carries a dileucine-based internalization signal that is normally masked by bound Lck; Nef displaces the kinase, exposes the motif, and links CD4 to the AP-2 clathrin adaptor complex, whose alpha, beta, mu and sigma subunits together with an ARF1- and V-ATPase-associated step drive endocytosis. Internalized receptor is then routed away from recycling toward lysosomal degradation, with COPI coatomer implicated in the final sorting. The pathway is a textbook case of a viral protein hijacking clathrin-mediated endocytosis and receptor trafficking for immune evasion."
