    "description": "Macrophage activation involved in immune response is the switch that turns a resting tissue macrophage into an effector cell: on encountering a cytokine, microbial product, or damage signal it changes shape, ramps up phagocytosis, and begins secreting inflammatory mediators that recruit and instruct other immune cells. This step sets the tone of innate immunity and of the tissue response that follows, and its misregulation drives chronic inflammation, sepsis pathology, and neurodegenerative microglial states. The genes cover the triggering inputs (IFNG, IL1B, IL33, HMGB1 as an alarmin, LBP delivering bacterial lipopolysaccharide), the receptors and adaptors that read them (IL1R1 with IL1RAP, TICAM1 downstream of Toll-like receptors, TREM2 signalling through TYROBP and SYK), and the phospholipase and kinase relays that convert them into transcription. TNF and IL1B are the principal outputs, while TREX1 and SBNO2 restrain the response."
