    "source": "go",
    "name": "positive regulation of leukocyte chemotaxis",
    "description": "positive regulation of leukocyte chemotaxis covers the signals that recruit white blood cells out of the bloodstream and steer them toward sites of infection, injury or tumour growth. It is how inflammation is localised: without it, host defence and wound healing fail, and when it is excessive it drives chronic inflammatory disease, atherosclerosis and leukocyte infiltration of tumours. The bulk of the activity is chemokine-based, with ligands such as CCL2, CCL5, CXCL8 and CXCL12 acting through G protein-coupled receptors including CCR2, CCR7, CX3CR1 and the complement receptors C3AR1 and C5AR1. Alarmins like HMGB1, lipid and peptide chemoattractants, and growth factors of the VEGF and CSF1 families add further recruiting cues. Downstream, ERK signalling, focal adhesion kinases, Rac GTPases and the WNK-OXSR1/STK39 module remodel the actin cytoskeleton and adhesion so cells polarise and crawl up the gradient."
