    "name": "complement activation (I)",
    "description": "complement activation (I) is the humoral arm of innate immunity that marks pathogens and damaged cells for destruction, drives inflammation, and clears immune complexes and debris from the circulation. Loss of it leaves recurrent pyogenic infection and lupus-like autoimmunity; loss of its brakes causes hemolysis and kidney injury, as in atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome and hereditary angioedema. The genes trace the classical route from the C1q recognition subunits through the C1r/C1s proteases and C2 to cleavage of C3, alongside the alternative amplification loop supplied by factor B and factor D. Cleavage fragments engage the C3a and C5a receptors on myeloid cells to recruit and activate leukocytes, while C7 contributes to the lytic terminal complex. Regulators including factor H, factor I, CR1 and C1 inhibitor restrict activation to intended surfaces and protect host tissue."
