    "source": "go",
    "name": "activation of NF-kappaB-inducing kinase activity",
    "description": "activation of NF-kappaB-inducing kinase activity describes how upstream receptors switch on NIK, the kinase that drives the non-canonical NF-kappaB route to processing of p100 into p52. This is the arm of NF-kappaB signalling that builds and maintains lymphoid tissue: lymphotoxin alpha/beta acting through the lymphotoxin beta receptor organises lymph node and spleen architecture and germinal centre stroma, and its loss leaves secondary lymphoid organs malformed. Tumour necrosis factor receptors, death receptors and Toll-like receptors feed into the same output, coupling infection and inflammatory cues to transcription of survival and chemokine genes. Activation works largely by relieving constitutive degradation, with the ubiquitin ligase ZFP91 stabilising NIK, while CARD10 and CARD14 scaffold receptor-proximal signalling in epithelium and skin, where gain-of-function mutations cause psoriasis. Sustained NIK activity is a feature of multiple myeloma and other lymphoid malignancies."
