    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of type I interferon-mediated signaling pathway",
    "description": "regulation of type I interferon-mediated signaling pathway tunes how strongly and how long cells respond to interferon-alpha and -beta, the cytokines that impose an antiviral state on infected and neighbouring tissue. Tight control matters in both directions: too little response permits viral replication, while failure to shut the response down causes interferonopathies such as Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome, seen with loss of the nucleic acid-metabolising enzymes ADAR, TREX1 and SAMHD1. The genes span sensing, transduction and termination -- cytosolic DNA and RNA sensors feeding STING1 and MAVS, the TBK1/IKBKE kinases that activate IRF3 and IRF7, and STAT2 downstream of the receptor. Restraint is imposed by phosphatases of the PTPN family, by ISG15 conjugation and its protease USP18, by TRIM and RNF ubiquitin ligases, and post-transcriptionally through m6A readers and mRNA decay factors, coupling innate immune output to autoimmune risk."
