   "exemplar": {
    "key": "reactome:R-HSA-446652",
    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Interleukin-1 family signaling",
    "description": "Interleukin-1 family signaling is the core alarm circuit of innate immunity: eleven related cytokines, including IL-1 dalpha/beta, IL-18, IL-33 and the IL-36 group, that announce infection, injury and cell death and drive fever, neutrophil recruitment, and downstream T helper responses. Its failure or over-activity underlies autoinflammatory fevers, gout, sepsis pathology, pustular psoriasis and asthma, and it is the target of anakinra and canakinumab. Most of these cytokines lack a secretion signal and are made as precursors, so activity is gated by inflammasome caspase-1 cleavage and gasdermin-D mediated release, and restrained by decoy receptors, antagonists such as IL1RN and IL18BP, and negative regulators including SIGIRR and IRAK3. Cytokine binding pairs a specific receptor with a shared accessory chain, assembling MyD88, IRAK kinases and TRAF6 to trigger ubiquitin-dependent IKK activation, proteasomal degradation of IκB, NF-κB transcription, and parallel p38, JNK and ERK output."
   },
