    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Toll Like Receptor 10 (TLR10) Cascade",
    "description": "Toll Like Receptor 10 (TLR10) Cascade describes innate immune signalling through one of the least understood Toll-like receptors, expressed mainly on B cells and dendritic cells as a heavily N-glycosylated surface protein that homodimerizes and can pair with TLR1 and TLR2. Its ligands remain undefined, but engagement recruits the MyD88 adaptor and the shared downstream module used by most TLRs: IRAK kinases, Pellino ubiquitin ligases, TRAF6-directed polyubiquitin chains, and the TAK1-TAB complex, which branches into IKK-driven degradation of IkB inhibitors to release NF-kB, and into the p38, JNK and ERK cascades that activate AP-1, CREB, ELK1 and MEF2 transcription factors. The output is transcription of inflammatory cytokines and immune activation genes, tuned by DUSP phosphatases, PP2A and IkB-related brakes. Because TLR10 can restrain responses from other TLRs, it contributes to setting the threshold and resolution of inflammation rather than only amplifying it."
