    "source": "go",
    "name": "inflammatory response",
    "description": "Inflammatory response is the body's immediate defensive reaction to infection or tissue injury, producing the local vasodilation, plasma leakage and leukocyte influx that clear pathogens and damaged cells and set the stage for repair. Without it, hosts cannot contain microbes; when it fails to resolve, the result is chronic inflammatory and autoimmune disease, fibrosis and septic shock. Pattern-recognition receptors such as the Toll-like receptors, NOD proteins and NLRP inflammasome sensors detect microbial and damage signals and route through MyD88, IRAK kinases, MAP kinases and NF-kB to induce cytokines including TNF, IL-1, IL-6 and IL-17 family members. Chemokines and their receptors direct neutrophils, monocytes and lymphocytes along selectin- and integrin-dependent adhesion routes, while eicosanoids, complement, kinins and acute-phase proteins amplify the response. Caspase-1-driven pyroptosis, negative regulators such as A20 and IL-10, and lipid mediator switching enforce resolution."
