    "name": "HALLMARK_INFLAMMATORY_RESPONSE",
    "description": "Inflammatory response is the coordinated reaction of tissue to infection or injury: recognition of danger, recruitment of leukocytes, and the vascular and metabolic changes that support them. It is what clears pathogens and initiates repair, and its failure to resolve underlies sepsis, atherosclerosis, arthritis and inflammatory bowel disease. The genes trace the full arc — pattern-recognition receptors such as TLR2, CD14, NOD2 and the NLRP3 inflammasome that senses cytosolic danger; NF-κB components RELA and NFKB1 that convert these signals into transcription; the resulting cytokines IL1B, IL6, TNF-family ligands and interferon-driven effectors; and chemokines with their receptors that direct neutrophils, monocytes and lymphocytes into tissue. Adhesion molecules SELE and ICAM1, complement and formyl-peptide receptors, prostaglandin and sphingolipid mediators, and endothelial factors including F3 and SERPINE1 couple immune activation to changes in blood flow, permeability and coagulation, while IL10 restrains the response."
