    "source": "go",
    "name": "antimicrobial humoral immune response mediated by antimicrobial peptide",
    "description": "antimicrobial humoral immune response mediated by antimicrobial peptide is the chemical arm of innate defence at wet surfaces: small secreted peptides in mucus, saliva, sweat, tears, gut fluid and blood that kill or disable bacteria, fungi and viruses within minutes of contact, long before lymphocytes respond. It keeps epithelial barriers of the skin, airway, gut and urogenital tract colonised by commensals rather than pathogens, and its failure underlies recurrent skin and mucosal infection, while dysregulated output drives psoriasis, Crohn's disease and other inflammatory conditions. Effectors include alpha- and beta-defensins from neutrophils and Paneth cells, cathelicidin, lactoferrin, histatins, RegIII lectins, S100 proteins, peptidoglycan recognition proteins and RNases, which permeabilise membranes or sequester nutrient metals; hepcidin additionally links this response to systemic iron withholding. Many chemokines act dually as direct microbicides and as recruiters of phagocytes, and IL-17 signalling induces the epithelial peptide programme."
