    "source": "btm",
    "name": "enriched in B cells (I)",
    "description": "enriched in B cells (I) is a transcriptional signature of the B lymphocyte lineage: the set of genes expressed far more highly in B cells than in other blood cells, useful for identifying B cell content in tissues and tumours and for tracking B cell depletion or expansion. Its members span the antigen receptor itself and its proximal signalling apparatus — surface immunoglobulin heavy and light chains, the CD79A/CD79B module, CD19, CD22, BLK, BANK1 and STAP1 — together with the transcription factors PAX5, EBF1, SPIB and TCF4 that commit progenitors to the lineage and maintain B cell identity. Also represented are co-receptors and inhibitory regulators (CD72, BTLA, FCER2, complement receptor CR2), the follicular homing receptor CXCR5, and markers of immature and naive stages such as VPREB3 and TCL1A. Loss of these programs abolishes antibody-mediated immunity; their dysregulation underlies B cell lymphomas and autoimmunity."
