    "source": "btm",
    "name": "antigen processing and presentation",
    "description": "antigen processing and presentation is how cells display fragments of proteins and lipids on their surface so that T cells can inspect them, the step that lets the adaptive immune system distinguish self from pathogen. Without it, helper T cells are never primed, antibody responses fail, and tumour or microbial antigens go unseen. The genes centre on the MHC class II arm: HLA-DP and HLA-DQ heterodimers load peptides in the endosomal compartment of dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells, while the HLA-DM chaperone catalyses removal of the placeholder CLIP peptide and edits the repertoire toward stable, high-affinity ligands. CD1D extends the same logic to lipid antigens recognised by invariant NKT cells, and WDFY4 is required for cross-presentation of cell-associated antigen by conventional dendritic cells to CD8 T cells. Variation here dominates genetic risk for autoimmune disease."
