    "description": "anatomical structure formation involved in morphogenesis covers the developmental steps by which a recognizable rudiment first takes shape from unpatterned tissue, and it is the process that turns an embryo's cell fate decisions into organs with form: heart chambers, somites, tooth enamel, kidney nephrons, myelinated nerves, blood and lymphatic vessels. Its failure produces congenital malformation rather than absence of cells. The genes span the standard morphogenetic toolkit -- Wnt, BMP/TGF-beta/Nodal, Notch, Hedgehog, FGF and VEGF/angiopoietin signalling read out by tissue-specific transcription factors such as TBX5, GATA4, MYOD1, PITX2 and HOX and SOX families -- acting on cell adhesion, integrin-matrix contacts, collagen and metalloproteinase remodelling, actomyosin contractility, planar polarity and ciliary function. Tube formation, sprouting angiogenesis, myoblast fusion, epithelial-mesenchymal transition and cell migration are the recurring cellular manoeuvres, with abundant microRNA regulation tuning their timing."
