    "name": "endodermal cell differentiation",
    "description": "endodermal cell differentiation is the specification of the innermost germ layer during gastrulation, the step that commits pluripotent epiblast cells to the lineage that will build gut, liver, pancreas, lung and thyroid. Without it no endoderm-derived organ forms, and its regulators are the same signals used to direct stem cells toward hepatocytes or beta cells in vitro. Nodal/activin signalling acting through EOMES, MIXL1, SOX17, GATA6 and HNF1B drives the transcriptional switch, while pluripotency factors POU5F1, NANOG and SOX2 must be dismantled and DKK1 tunes Wnt/beta-catenin input. Chromatin and elongation machinery, including the PAF1 complex, SETD2 and macroH2A, make the new programme heritable. The prominent collagens, laminins, fibronectin, integrins and matrix metalloproteinases reflect the cell-shape changes, basement membrane remodelling and migration by which nascent endoderm ingresses and forms an epithelial sheet."
