    "description": "Regulation of cell fate specification controls the decisions by which an uncommitted embryonic or progenitor cell adopts one identity rather than another, setting the germ layers, body axes and organ primordia that all later development builds on. Its failure produces patterning defects and, in adult tissues, loss of the boundary between stem and differentiated states that underlies tumour formation. Two kinds of input converge here. Secreted signals and their receptors — WNT3A, FZD7 and the antagonists DKK1 and SFRP2, BMP receptor signalling, FGF2 through FGFR1 — provide positional information to which cells respond. Chromatin machinery then makes the response durable: the nucleosome remodelling and deacetylase complex, built from CHD3/CHD4, HDAC1/2, MTA and MBD3 subunits with RBBP4/7, silences alternative programmes so that lineage factors such as SOX17 and MESP1 can lock in mesodermal and endodermal fate."
