    "name": "HALLMARK_PROTEIN_SECRETION",
    "description": "Protein secretion is the membrane-traffic system that moves newly made proteins from the endoplasmic reticulum through the Golgi to the cell surface or lysosome, and retrieves and recycles membrane along the way. It is what allows cells to deliver hormones, enzymes, adhesion molecules and receptors to the right compartment, and its failure underlies lysosomal storage diseases, defective coagulation factor export and disrupted receptor signalling. The genes trace each step: COPII components for ER exit, COPI and ARF/GBF1 machinery for retrograde and intra-Golgi transport, tethering by USO1, COG and ZW10, and SNARE pairs such as BET1, GOSR2, YKT6, VAMP3 and STX16 that fuse each vesicle with its target. Clathrin adaptors, RAB GTPases and ESCRT proteins extend the network into endocytosis, mannose-6-phosphate-dependent lysosomal enzyme sorting, endosome maturation and exosome release."
