    "name": "somitogenesis",
    "description": "Somitogenesis is the process that carves the vertebrate embryo's paraxial mesoderm into a repeating series of blocks, the somites, which go on to form the vertebrae, ribs, skeletal muscle and dermis. Its regularity sets the body's segmental plan, and its disruption produces congenital vertebral malformations and rib fusions such as spondylocostal dysostosis. Timing comes from a segmentation clock built on oscillating Notch signalling, with HES7, LFNG, DLL1 and DLL3 and the glycosyltransferases that modify Notch receptors setting the period, read out against opposing Wnt and FGF gradients from the tail bud. TBX6, MSGN1 and TBXT maintain the presomitic mesoderm, while MESP2 and the RIPPLY repressors define each future boundary and impose anterior-posterior polarity within a somite. Downstream transcription factors including PAX-family partners, MEOX and MYF5 then commit somite compartments to sclerotome and myotome fates."
