    "name": "regulation of microvillus assembly",
    "description": "regulation of microvillus assembly controls how cells build the dense arrays of actin-cored surface protrusions that create absorptive brush borders in the intestine and kidney, and it determines whether that apical surface forms in the right place at all. Loss of this control underlies microvillus inclusion disease and cholestatic intestinal failure, where enterocytes internalise their brush border and cannot absorb nutrients. The genes describe a Rap GTPase module — activating exchange factors and a GAP that sets Rap1/Rap2 output — feeding into the STE20 kinases STK26 and TNIK, which phosphorylate the ezrin-based apical scaffold and specify where microvilli grow. Alongside these are effectors acting directly on the actin core and glycocalyx, including the bundling protein fascin and the apical marker podocalyxin, plus the aminophospholipid flippase ATP8B1 that maintains apical membrane lipid asymmetry. The pathway thus links polarity signalling to actin cytoskeletal architecture and epithelial transport function."
