    "name": "regulation of cell-matrix adhesion",
    "description": "regulation of cell-matrix adhesion controls how tightly and how transiently a cell grips the extracellular matrix, the decision that determines whether it stays anchored, migrates, or dies by anoikis. It underlies wound healing, angiogenesis, immune cell trafficking and tissue architecture, and its dysregulation permits tumour invasion, metastasis and fibrosis. The core machinery is the integrin adhesion complex: talin, kindlins and vinculin link receptors such as ITGB3 to actin, while FAK, PTK2B and SRC transduce engagement into survival and cytoskeletal signalling. Rho-family GTPases with their GAPs and effectors, including RHOA, RAC1, DLC1 and ROCK kinases, tune contractility and adhesion turnover, and phosphatases, PTEN-PI3K signalling and microtubule-tethering proteins set the timing of focal adhesion assembly and disassembly. Proteases, matricellular proteins and guidance cues such as thrombospondin, MMP14, semaphorins and ephrins modify the matrix itself, coupling adhesion strength to the surrounding environment."
