    "description": "positive regulation of cell adhesion mediated by integrin covers the signals that switch integrins into their high-affinity, ligand-binding state and strengthen the resulting attachments to extracellular matrix and to other cells. This is how leukocytes arrest on inflamed endothelium, how platelets aggregate through activated ITGB3 to stop bleeding, and how adherent cells build the traction needed for migration and tissue assembly; loss of it produces bleeding disorders, immunodeficiency and defective wound repair. The genes span the inside-out arm, in which chemokines such as CCL5 and CXCL13, antigen receptor signalling through CD3E, SYK and the adaptor SKAP1, and purinergic and mechanical inputs including P2RY12 and PIEZO1 converge on kindlin-family activators and CIB1, and the outside-in arm, where focal adhesion kinase and RAC3 consolidate adhesion and cytoskeletal coupling. Growth factor and TGF-beta signals set the adhesive tone more slowly, tying the pathway to development and fibrosis."
