    "description": "regulation of extracellular matrix organization controls how much matrix a tissue builds, how it is cross-linked and assembled, and how fast it is broken down again. This balance determines tissue stiffness and architecture during development and wound healing, and its loss drives fibrosis of lung, liver, kidney and heart as well as the stromal remodelling that lets tumours invade. TGF-beta signalling through its receptors and SMAD3/SMAD4 is the dominant pro-matrix input, opposed by protease inhibitors such as TIMP3 and RECK that restrain metalloproteinase activity. Collagen and laminin receptors including the discoidin domain kinases DDR1/DDR2 and integrins let cells sense the matrix they have made and adjust output, while transcription factors such as SOX9, RUNX1 and PPARG set matrix programmes by cell type. Numerous microRNAs, notably the miR-29 family, tune collagen and basement membrane gene expression post-transcriptionally."
