    "source": "go",
    "name": "cell-cell junction organization",
    "description": "cell-cell junction organization builds, remodels and takes apart the physical contacts that hold cells together in a tissue, and it is what makes an epithelium a barrier rather than a collection of cells. Without it, sheets cannot seal, apical-basal polarity is lost, and cells detach and become invasive, as happens when E-cadherin is silenced by SNAI1/SNAI2 during epithelial-mesenchymal transition. The genes span the major junction classes: classical and desmosomal cadherins with their catenin, plakin and plakophilin plaques, claudins and occludin with ZO-family scaffolds at tight junctions, nectins and JAM proteins, and connexins forming gap junctions for direct intercellular coupling. Assembly is coupled to the actin cytoskeleton through vinculin, talin and Rho-family GTPases, and is directed by PAR/Crumbs polarity complexes and by TGF-beta and Wnt signals. Consequences of failure include leaky endothelium, blistering skin disease, arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy and defective glomerular filtration."
