    "name": "cell cell adhesion",
    "description": "cell cell adhesion is the machinery that binds epithelial cells to one another and to their basement membrane, creating the sealed, polarized sheets that line skin, gut, lung and glands. Without it epithelia lose barrier function and cohesion: cells detach, tissues blister, and tumour cells acquire the migratory, invasive behaviour of epithelial-mesenchymal transition, which is why loss of E-cadherin is a classic marker of carcinoma progression. Three junction types are represented. Classical cadherins CDH1 and CDH3, linked through junctional plakoglobin to the cytoskeleton, mediate calcium-dependent homophilic contact; claudins CLDN3, CLDN4 and CLDN7 build the tight-junction strands that set paracellular permeability and apicobasal polarity; and laminin-332 with integrins β6β4 anchors basal keratinocytes to the matrix via hemidesmosomes. Mutations in the laminin-332 chains cause junctional epidermolysis bullosa, showing how directly adhesion translates into tissue integrity."
