    "description": "nephron development builds the kidney's filtering unit, from the first inductive signals between ureteric epithelium and metanephric mesenchyme through to a patterned tubule with glomerulus, proximal segment, loop and distal nephron. It sets final nephron number, so its disruption produces renal hypodysplasia, congenital nephrotic syndrome and a lifelong deficit in filtration reserve. Nephron progenitors are held in a self-renewing state by SIX2, WT1, PAX2 and SALL1 and are recruited by WNT9B/WNT4 signalling, with GDNF-RET, BMP, Hedgehog, Notch and Hippo/YAP inputs coordinating branching, mesenchymal-to-epithelial transition and proximo-distal segmentation. Later steps assemble the specialised structures that make filtration possible: podocyte slit diaphragm components such as NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL and CD2AP, glomerular basement membrane collagen IV and laminins, and PDGFB-driven recruitment of mesangial and endothelial cells. Segment-specific transporters and channels mark terminal differentiation of the mature tubule."
