    "description": "Glomerular mesangial cell development describes the formation and maturation of the contractile support cells that sit between glomerular capillary loops in the kidney. Mesangial cells anchor the capillary tuft, secrete and remodel its matrix, and adjust filtration surface area by contracting; without them the developing glomerulus fails to split into discrete capillary loops and forms a dilated, non-functional balloon, and their later expansion and matrix overproduction is the lesion of diabetic and other progressive glomerulopathies. Recruitment depends on endothelium-derived PDGFB acting on mesangial progenitors, with Notch signalling and the transcription factor FOXC2 shaping podocyte and mesangial fate during nephron patterning. Acquisition of a smooth-muscle-like phenotype, marked by ACTA2, gives the mature cell its contractile and tone-regulating capacity, linking this developmental programme to control of glomerular haemodynamics and filtration."
