    "source": "go",
    "name": "blood vessel endothelial cell differentiation",
    "description": "Blood vessel endothelial cell differentiation is the process by which an uncommitted progenitor acquires the flattened, junction-rich phenotype of the cells lining the vascular lumen, and with it the identity of artery, vein or capillary. It sets the template for a functional circulation: without correct specification, vessels fail to separate arterial from venous fate, remodel poorly, and form malformations such as arteriovenous shunts or cavernous lesions. Notch signalling dominates the mechanism, with DLL1 ligand activating NOTCH1 and the RBPJ transcription complex to induce the HEY1 and HEY2 repressors that enforce arterial character, while NRP1 couples VEGF input to the same decision. CCM2 and TMEM100 act on junctional stability and endothelial quiescence downstream, and microRNAs including miR-34a and miR-10a tune the transition. The pathway sits within vasculogenesis and angiogenesis and underlies vascular integrity throughout life."
