    "name": "regulation of Notch signaling pathway",
    "description": "regulation of Notch signaling pathway controls how strongly and how long the Notch receptors signal, tuning a juxtacrine system that decides cell fate between neighbouring cells. Because Notch output sets the balance between progenitor maintenance and differentiation, its regulation shapes neurogenesis, somite segmentation, angiogenesis and arterial-venous specification, T cell development and gut epithelial renewal; loss of restraint drives leukaemia and Alagille- or CADASIL-type vascular disease. Control is exerted at every step: glycosyltransferases such as POFUT1, POGLUT1 and the fringe enzymes modify the receptor to bias ligand preference, ADAM10 with its tetraspanin partners performs the activating cleavage, ligands including DLL1, DLL4, JAG1 and the non-signalling DLK1 set input strength, ubiquitin ligases DTX1, FBXW7 and WWP2 limit receptor lifetime, and HES/HEY repressors plus NRARP close feedback loops. Crosstalk with BMP, WNT, YAP and STAT3 integrates Notch with other fate decisions."
