    "name": "regulation of osteoblast differentiation",
    "description": "Regulation of osteoblast differentiation controls how mesenchymal progenitors commit to becoming bone-forming osteoblasts rather than adipocytes or chondrocytes, setting the pace of skeletal growth, fracture healing and lifelong bone mass. When it is tuned wrongly the result is osteoporosis, high bone mass syndromes, craniosynostosis or heterotopic ossification. The core is a transcriptional switch built on RUNX2, OSX-associated cofactors and ATF4, driven by BMP ligands acting through type I and II receptors and SMAD1/5, and by WNT ligands acting through LRP5 and β-catenin. Both arms are held in check by dedicated antagonists — noggin, chordin, gremlin, SMAD6, SFRPs, AXIN2 — and by inhibitory factors such as TWIST1, ID proteins and PPARG, which diverts progenitors to fat. Notch, hedgehog, IGF1, FGF and Hippo effectors YAP/TAZ feed in, and a large complement of microRNAs sets the threshold post-transcriptionally."
