    "description": "Chondrocyte development covers the maturation of a committed cartilage cell through proliferation, hypertrophy and matrix production, the sequence that builds the growth plate and thereby determines the length and shape of every endochondral bone. When it fails, the result is skeletal dysplasia, disproportionate short stature or premature joint degeneration. SOX9 sustains the chondrocytic program while RUNX2 and MSX2 drive the switch to hypertrophy, and the pace of that switch is set by PTHLH acting against FGF18, BMP receptor and TGF-beta receptor signalling, with WNT modulators such as AXIN2 and SFRP2 tuning the balance. The rest of the program is secretory: collagen XI and XXVII, matrilin, COMP and the enzymes that synthesise and sulfate proteoglycan chains build a cartilage matrix, whose folding and export depend on SERPINH1 and PERK-mediated stress signalling. Matrix remodelling by sulfatases and SMPD3 prepares hypertrophic cartilage for mineralisation and replacement by bone."
