    "name": "negative regulation of bone mineralization",
    "description": "Negative regulation of bone mineralization is the braking system that keeps hydroxyapatite deposition confined to bone and prevents it from occurring too fast, too much, or in the wrong tissue. Its failure produces ectopic calcification of arteries and soft tissue, while excessive braking underlies rickets and osteomalacia. Circulating and matrix inhibitors do much of the work: fetuin-A binds nascent calcium-phosphate mineral in serum, ENPP1 generates pyrophosphate that poisons crystal growth, and salivary statherin blocks precipitation at mineralized surfaces. Endocrine control runs through parathyroid hormone and the bone-derived phosphaturic hormone FGF23, which lowers phosphate availability. Other members act on the osteoblast itself, with BMP antagonism by gremlin, SOX9-driven chondrogenic commitment, and hypoxic and inflammatory signals through HIF1A and chemokine receptors restraining matrix maturation. Together these set the balance between skeletal mineral accrual and vascular calcification."
