    "name": "regulation of tooth mineralization",
    "description": "regulation of tooth mineralization controls how much calcium phosphate is deposited into enamel and dentin, and how well ordered the resulting crystal is. It is what gives teeth their hardness and wear resistance; when it fails the result is amelogenesis imperfecta, hypomineralized or hypoplastic enamel, and dentin defects seen in hereditary rickets and syndromic craniofacial disease. Ameloblasts secrete a provisional matrix of amelogenin, enamelin and amelotin that templates crystal growth, and proteinases such as MMP20 then degrade that matrix so mineral can expand to fill the space. Ion handling supports the same end: anion exchange and CFTR-dependent transport buffer the protons released as apatite forms, while DMP1 nucleates mineral in dentin. Transcriptional and signalling inputs including TFAP2A, BCOR and WNT6 set ameloblast and odontoblast identity upstream of secretion, tying this process to tooth morphogenesis and biomineralization generally."
