    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Defective F8 binding to the cell membrane",
    "description": "Defective F8 binding to the cell membrane describes a molecular lesion in blood coagulation: mutant factor VIII that can no longer dock onto phospholipid surfaces, and therefore cannot assemble the intrinsic clotting machinery where it is needed. Normally thrombin-activated FVIIIa binds phosphatidylserine exposed on activated platelets through its C1 and C2 discoidin domains, serving as the cofactor that concentrates factor IXa and accelerates factor X activation by orders of magnitude. Hemophilia A variants clustered in these membrane-binding domains, including substitutions and deletions such as A2220P and Q2330P, leave the protein expressed but functionally inert, so tenase complex formation fails and thrombin generation is inadequate. The clinical consequence is X-linked hemophilia A with spontaneous joint and soft-tissue bleeding, illustrating how coagulation depends on correct localisation of enzyme complexes to membranes rather than on enzyme activity alone."
