    "source": "btm",
    "name": "mitotic cell division",
    "description": "mitotic cell division is the machinery that attaches chromosomes to spindle microtubules and delays anaphase until every attachment is correct, so that each daughter cell inherits one exact copy of the genome. When it fails, chromosomes missegregate and cells become aneuploid, a hallmark of tumours and a cause of embryonic lethality. The genes describe the kinetochore itself, from the CENP-A-marked centromeric chromatin and its inner constitutive components through the outer NDC80/NUF2 microtubule-binding complex and the SKA subunits that stabilise attachment. Layered on this are the spindle assembly checkpoint proteins BUB1, MAD2L1 and the RZZ components, which restrain CDC20 and hence the anaphase-promoting complex, and the error-correcting kinases Aurora B and PLK1 acting with the chromosomal passenger complex. Depolymerising kinesins and shugoshin-mediated cohesion protection complete the control of chromosome movement and separation."
