    "name": "chromatin organization",
    "description": "chromatin organization covers the assembly and continual remodeling of the nucleosomal packaging of DNA, the layer of regulation that decides which genes a cell can read and how faithfully its genome is copied and repaired. It defines cell identity and differentiation, and its disruption underlies cancers, imprinting disorders and neurodevelopmental syndromes. The genes span the core and variant histones themselves, histone chaperones such as ASF1 and the CAF-1 subunits that deposit them behind the replication fork, and ATP-dependent remodelers of the SWI/SNF, ISWI, CHD and INO80 families that slide and evict nucleosomes. Layered on top are the writers, erasers and readers of histone marks — acetyltransferases and HDACs, SET-domain and PRDM methyltransferases, JmjC and LSD demethylases, bromo- and chromodomain readers — together with DNA methylation machinery, Polycomb complexes, cohesin and CTCF-based genome folding, and specialized centromeric and germline-specific packaging."
