    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of attachment of mitotic spindle microtubules to kinetochore",
    "description": "regulation of attachment of mitotic spindle microtubules to kinetochore controls how tightly and how correctly chromosomes are gripped by the spindle before they are pulled apart, and thereby determines whether the two daughter cells inherit equal genomes. Errors here produce lagging chromosomes, aneuploidy and the chromosomal instability characteristic of tumours. The core activity is the chromosomal passenger complex — Aurora B kinase with INCENP, survivin and borealin — which localises to inner centromeres and phosphorylates outer kinetochore substrates to destabilise attachments that are not under proper bipolar tension, giving the cell repeated chances to correct syntelic and merotelic errors while the spindle assembly checkpoint delays anaphase. CDK1 sets the mitotic timing in which this operates, and acetyltransferase and sirtuin activities tune centromeric chromatin and passenger complex behaviour, linking error correction to chromatin state."
