    "description": "Cell Cycle, Mitotic is the complete program by which a cell duplicates its genome and partitions it equally into two daughters, the basis of tissue growth, renewal and development; its deregulation is the defining feature of cancer and its errors generate aneuploidy. Progression is timed by cyclin-dependent kinases paired with D, E, A and B cyclins, restrained by CDK inhibitors and the RB-E2F and DREAM transcriptional switches that decide whether a quiescent cell re-enters division. Downstream steps are represented in depth: licensing of origins by ORC, CDC6 and CDT1, MCM helicase loading and replisome-driven synthesis in S phase, WEE1/CDC25 control of CDK1 at G2/M, centrosome maturation and gamma-tubulin-nucleated spindle assembly, kinetochore and cohesin function under spindle checkpoint surveillance, nuclear envelope breakdown and reformation, and APC/C-driven ubiquitin-proteasome degradation of securin and cyclin B that triggers anaphase and cytokinesis."
