    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Establishment of Sister Chromatid Cohesion",
    "description": "Establishment of Sister Chromatid Cohesion is the step in S phase that converts the transient, dynamic loading of cohesin onto chromatin into a stable embrace of the two newly replicated sister chromatids. Without it sisters cannot be held together until anaphase, and chromosomes missegregate, producing aneuploidy; mutations in these components underlie cohesinopathies such as Roberts syndrome. Cohesin itself is a ring of SMC1A, SMC3 and RAD21 with a STAG1 or STAG2 subunit, and its residence on DNA reflects a tug of war between loading and the release activity of WAPL bound to PDS5A/PDS5B. Replication-coupled acetylation of SMC3 by ESCO1 and ESCO2 permits recruitment of sororin (CDCA5), which displaces WAPL and locks the ring in place. This licensing underpins faithful chromosome segregation, and also feeds into DNA repair and chromatin looping."
