    "source": "go",
    "name": "cellular senescence",
    "description": "Cellular senescence is the permanent exit from the cell cycle that cells adopt when stressed beyond repair, and it serves as a barrier to tumour formation while also driving tissue ageing. Oncogene activation, telomere erosion, oxidative damage and DNA double-strand breaks converge on the ATM-p53-p21 axis and on the p16/p15-CDK4/6-RB arm, which together enforce arrest with hypophosphorylated RB and silenced proliferative genes. Chromatin changes make the state stable: heterochromatin writers such as SUV39H1, the sirtuins, and lamin A/C-dependent nuclear envelope remodelling, with progeroid lamin defects causing premature senescence. Stress kinase signalling through the p38 and JNK modules, cytoplasmic DNA sensing by cGAS, and a set of p53-responsive microRNAs including miR-34a tune entry and the accompanying inflammatory secretion. Loss of these controls permits immortalisation and transformation; their overactivity depletes stem cell pools and promotes degenerative disease."
