    "source": "go",
    "name": "mitotic recombination",
    "description": "Mitotic recombination is homologous recombination as it operates in dividing somatic cells, where its job is to repair double-strand breaks and collapsed replication forks using an intact sister chromatid as template. This is the high-fidelity arm of break repair, and its loss underlies chromosomal instability and cancer predisposition, most familiarly through BRCA2 deficiency and the sensitivity to PARP inhibition that follows. Mechanistically, the MRE11-RAD50 nuclease complex resects broken ends, mediators load RAD51 and its paralogues onto single-stranded DNA to drive strand invasion, and structure-specific nucleases such as MUS81-EME1 and GEN1 resolve the resulting joint molecules; the SMC5/6 complex with the NSMCE2 SUMO ligase restrains and disentangles recombination intermediates. Mismatch repair proteins police heteroduplex fidelity, while a telomere-associated branch supports recombination-based telomere maintenance in cells lacking telomerase activity."
