    "description": "Inhibition of DNA recombination at telomere is the protective packaging that stops chromosome ends from being mistaken for double-strand breaks. Telomeres look like broken DNA, and unless they are capped they recruit break-repair machinery, triggering damage signalling, senescence, and end-to-end fusions by non-homologous or microhomology-mediated joining. The shelterin complex — TRF1, TRF2, RAP1, TIN2, TPP1 and POT1 — binds telomeric repeats and blocks both recombination and checkpoint activation, while the ATRX-DAXX chaperone deposits histone H3.3 to keep telomeric chromatin repressive and limit transcription by RNA polymerase II of telomeric RNA. Loss of this restraint, especially ATRX inactivation, permits homologous recombination between telomeres and drives alternative lengthening of telomeres, a telomerase-independent route to replicative immortality used by roughly 5-15% of cancers. The pathway therefore sits at the junction of chromosome-end maintenance, chromatin state, and tumour suppression."
