    "name": "postsynaptic density organization",
    "description": "Postsynaptic density organization builds and remodels the dense protein lattice beneath the postsynaptic membrane of excitatory synapses, the platform that holds glutamate receptors in register with presynaptic release sites. Its integrity sets synaptic strength and the capacity for plasticity underlying learning and memory, and mutations in its scaffolds — SHANK3, SYNGAP1, DLG1, NRXN and NLGN family members — are among the most reproducible genetic causes of autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability. Assembly begins with trans-synaptic adhesion: neurexins pairing with neuroligins, LRRTM and SLITRK proteins, and receptor phosphatases PTPRD and PTPRS, which nucleate scaffold recruitment across the cleft. Multidomain scaffolds then tether receptors, signalling enzymes and Rho-family regulators such as OPHN1 and GIT1 to the actin cytoskeleton, so that spine morphology, receptor number and downstream kinase signalling are coordinated during synapse maturation and activity-driven remodelling."
