    "description": "gamma-aminobutyric acid transport moves the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter across membranes: packaging it into synaptic vesicles, clearing it from the synaptic cleft, and shuttling it between neurons and glia. Without this traffic, inhibitory tone collapses or persists inappropriately, and the resulting excitation-inhibition imbalance manifests as epilepsy, spasticity and altered anxiety states; the plasma-membrane carriers are direct drug targets, as tiagabine's action on SLC6A1 shows. The vesicular inhibitory transporter SLC32A1 loads GABA and glycine for regulated release, while sodium- and chloride-coupled SLC6 family carriers in neurons and astrocytes terminate signalling and recycle transmitter toward degradation by the mitochondrial transaminase ABAT. Release and reuptake are tuned by presynaptic scaffolds, calcium channel subunits, metabotropic GABA-B receptors and glial channels such as BEST1, linking transport capacity to synaptic strength and network rhythm generation."
