    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of neuron projection development",
    "description": "regulation of neuron projection development controls how far, how fast and in which direction neurons extend axons and dendrites, and therefore determines how the nervous system is wired. Its outputs are the growth cone's decisions during development and the capacity for neurite regrowth or collapse after injury, where inhibitory myelin signals through Nogo receptors and RTN4 block repair. Guidance cue families dominate the set — netrins and DCC, slits and ROBOs, semaphorins with plexins and neuropilins, ephrins with Eph receptors — acting alongside neurotrophins and their TRK receptors, Reelin–VLDLR/DAB1 signalling, and Wnt ligands. These converge on Rho-family GTPase regulators, PAK and LIMK kinases, CDK5, GSK3 and cofilin to remodel actin, and on MAPT, MAP1B and katanin-associated factors to reorganise microtubules. Disrupted regulation underlies cortical malformations, intellectual disability and autism-spectrum disorders, and constrains recovery from central nervous system damage."
