    "name": "regulation of dendrite morphogenesis",
    "description": "regulation of dendrite morphogenesis controls how far and how elaborately a neuron's dendritic arbor grows, and thereby how many synaptic inputs that neuron can receive. Arbor size and branching pattern define neuronal cell types and set the wiring capacity of cortical and cerebellar circuits; when this control fails, dendrites are stunted or mis-oriented and the result is intellectual disability, epilepsy and autism-spectrum phenotypes, as seen with CDKL5 and IL1RAPL1 mutations. The genes converge on several regulatory layers: extracellular guidance cues and receptors such as Reelin with its receptor LRP8, ephrin receptors and semaphorin signalling; cytoskeletal effectors including drebrin, BAIAP2 and microtubule-associated proteins; calcium-dependent relays through CaMKII, calcineurin and NFATC4 that couple activity to growth; ubiquitin ligases of the NEDD4 and HECW families that restrain branching; and transcriptional programs driven by CUX1/CUX2 and NR2E1. Local mRNA regulation by Staufen and caprin proteins targets this growth to individual branches."
