    "description": "Central nervous system neuron differentiation is how progenitors in the neural tube and its derivatives stop dividing and become neurons with a defined identity, position and axonal target. It builds the cortex, spinal cord, midbrain dopaminergic and cholinergic populations and the interneurons that balance them; its disruption underlies lissencephaly, holoprosencephaly, intellectual disability and epilepsy. Patterning signals -- Sonic hedgehog, Wnts, FGF8 and BMPs -- set dorsoventral and anteroposterior coordinates that are read out by homeodomain and bHLH transcription factors such as PAX6, OLIG2, ASCL1, NKX2-1, ISL1 and NEUROD family members, while FOXG1, TBR1, BCL11B and FEZF2 assign cortical layer and projection identity. Downstream, guidance receptors and ligands including DCC, ROBO/SLIT, Ephrin receptors, plexins and neuropilins, together with cytoskeletal and migration machinery such as LIS1, NDEL1, CDK5 and tubulin, direct migration, neurite outgrowth and synapse formation."
