    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of gliogenesis",
    "description": "Regulation of gliogenesis governs how many astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and microglia a developing or injured nervous system produces, and when. It sets the balance between neuronal and glial fates in neural progenitors, and its output determines myelination capacity, astrocytic support of synapses, and the reactive gliosis and glial scarring that follow injury; misregulation contributes to glioma and to demyelinating disease. Control is layered. Notch effectors such as HES1 and HES5, together with polycomb and HDAC-mediated chromatin repression, hold progenitors from premature differentiation, while SHH, BMP, WNT/CTNNB1 and LIF/IL6-family cytokine signalling through IL6ST push progenitors toward astrocytic or oligodendroglial identity. Lineage transcription factors including OLIG2, SOX10, NKX2-2 and MYRF then drive oligodendrocyte maturation and myelin gene expression. Post-transcriptional regulators QKI, LIN28A, DICER1 and a large complement of microRNAs tune these decisions, and inflammatory signals such as TNF and IL1B redirect them during disease."
