    "source": "go",
    "name": "neuron projection regeneration",
    "description": "neuron projection regeneration is the regrowth of axons and dendrites after injury, the process that determines whether a damaged nerve reconnects with its target or leaves permanent deficit. It explains why peripheral nerves recover while central axons largely do not: the genes include the myelin-associated inhibitors MAG and OMG together with their Nogo receptors and the chondroitin sulfate proteoglycans of the glial scar, which actively suppress regrowth, alongside permissive cues such as CNTF, pleiotrophin, laminin and tenascin. Intracellular arms run from neurotrophin receptors and integrin-like adhesion through JAK2 and the BRAF-MEK cascade to regeneration-associated transcription factors JUN and KLF4, while MAP1B, neurofilaments and axonal transport adaptors rebuild the growth cone itself. Receptor phosphatases PTPRS and PTPRF read the inhibitory matrix. Manipulating this balance is the basis of strategies for spinal cord injury, optic nerve damage and peripheral neuropathy repair."
