    "source": "go",
    "name": "cortical microtubule organization",
    "description": "Cortical microtubule organization builds and remodels the microtubule arrays that lie just under the plasma membrane, the layer where the cytoskeleton is coupled to cell adhesions and to the cell surface. This coupling sets where a cell places its spindle, how it polarizes, and how it steers during migration; when it fails, spindles misorient and tissue architecture and axon guidance suffer, as in the congenital fibrosis of the extraocular muscles caused by KIF21A mutation and the neuronal migration defects of lissencephaly from PAFAH1B1 loss. Mechanistically, KANK1 links talin at integrin adhesions to liprin-containing cortical platforms that capture and stabilize microtubule plus ends, while ezrin and DLG1 anchor the array to membrane and polarity complexes and KIF21A restrains excess growth. The result is a mechanically responsive interface between microtubules, adhesion sites, and cortical signalling."
