    "description": "regulation of protein localization to cell cortex controls which proteins are recruited to the membrane-associated cortical layer, and thereby sets the geometry of cell division and the polarity of epithelia. Its output is spindle orientation: an LGN-Gαi-NuMA module docks dynein at defined cortical sites so that astral microtubules pull the spindle into position, deciding whether daughter cells divide symmetrically within the plane of an epithelium or asymmetrically to generate distinct fates. Membrane skeleton proteins of the band 4.1 family and the spinophilin scaffold anchor these complexes to cortical actin, while mitotic phosphorylation by PLK1 and inhibition by SAPCD2 restrict recruitment to the correct cell cycle stage and cortical region. Loss of this control produces misoriented divisions, disorganised epithelial and neuroepithelial architecture, and defects in progenitor self-renewal during development."
