    "name": "Assembly of the 9+2 motile cilia",
    "description": "Assembly of the 9+2 motile cilia builds the beating cilia that line the airways, brain ventricles, and reproductive tract, where their coordinated strokes clear mucus, circulate cerebrospinal fluid, and move gametes. Beyond the nine microtubule doublets and central pair, motile axonemes require dynein arms, radial spokes, and nexin links, and multiciliated cells must first amplify hundreds of centrioles through deuterosomes. The genes here represent the transcriptional and post-transcriptional program that licenses this fate: GMNC and MCIDAS relieve Geminin-mediated restraint and partner with E2F4/E2F5 and TFDP1 to drive centriole amplification through DEUP1 and CCNO, while FOXJ1, RFX2/RFX3, MYB, TP73, and Grainyhead factors switch on axonemal and motility genes. Notch signalling limits how many progenitors adopt this fate, and miR-34/449 families acting through Argonaute complexes reinforce the commitment. Failure produces primary ciliary dyskinesia, with chronic airway infection, laterality defects, and infertility."
