    "source": "go",
    "name": "auditory receptor cell morphogenesis",
    "description": "Auditory receptor cell morphogenesis builds the shape of the cochlear hair cell, above all the staircase bundle of actin-filled stereocilia that converts sound-driven fluid motion into receptor current. Getting this geometry right is what makes hearing possible: mutations in these genes cause congenital deafness and, when the eye is also affected, Usher syndrome. The genes describe how the bundle is assembled and held together — cadherin-based tip and lateral links such as CDH23 and STRC, the scaffolds USH1C, WHRN and PDZD7 that anchor them, an unconventional myosin that transports cargo along stereocilia, and actin cross-linkers including PLS1 and TRIOBP that set rootlet and core stiffness. Planar cell polarity and cytoskeletal regulators orient the bundle within the sensory epithelium, while the tectorial membrane component TECTA couples it mechanically. The result is a polarized mechanotransducing cell whose form is inseparable from its function."
