    "name": "vesicle-mediated transport",
    "description": "vesicle-mediated transport is the cell's internal courier system: cargo is packaged into membrane-bounded vesicles, budded from a donor compartment, moved along cytoskeletal tracks, and fused with a target membrane. Nearly every aspect of cell physiology depends on it — secretion of hormones, antibodies and neurotransmitters, uptake of nutrients and receptors by endocytosis, delivery of lysosomal enzymes, recycling of plasma membrane transporters, and antigen presentation. The genes span the whole itinerary: COPII and COPI coats and TRAPP/Golgi tethers for early secretory traffic, clathrin with AP adaptors and dynamins for budding and scission, Rab GTPases with their GEFs and GAPs specifying compartment identity, SNAREs, synaptotagmins and NSF driving fusion, and retromer, WASH, ESCRT and HOPS complexes sorting cargo toward recycling or degradation. Loss of individual components causes neurodegeneration, immune deficiency, pigmentation and lysosomal storage disorders, reflecting how specialised secretory and phagocytic cells are."
