    "source": "go",
    "name": "mRNA 3'-end processing",
    "description": "mRNA 3'-end processing builds the mature 3' end of a transcript, cleaving the nascent RNA and adding the poly(A) tail that determines whether a message is exported, translated, and how long it survives. Every protein-coding gene depends on it, and choice among alternative cleavage sites reshapes the 3' UTR landscape, changing which microRNA and RNA-binding-protein sites a transcript retains; misregulation of this choice is a hallmark of proliferating and cancerous cells. The core machinery is the cleavage and polyadenylation complex — CPSF subunits with WDR33 and FIP1L1 recognising the AAUAAA signal, CstF binding downstream sequence, symplekin scaffolding, and PAPOLA extending the tail. Coupling to transcription runs through the RNA polymerase II CTD via CDK9, SSU72 and the PAF1 complex, so termination and processing are decided together. A separate branch, using LSM10/LSM11 and SLBP, generates the non-polyadenylated histone mRNAs of S phase."
