    "name": "Translation",
    "description": "Translation is the decoding of mRNA into protein, the step at which genetic information becomes the working machinery of the cell, and it consumes a large share of cellular energy and biosynthetic capacity. Cytosolic ribosomal subunits, aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, and initiation, elongation and release factors carry out initiation at the start codon, cyclic peptide-bond formation, and stop-codon-triggered release and recycling; cap-binding and eIF2 regulation make this the main point at which growth signals and stress restrain protein output. Included are the co-translational branches: signal recognition particle capture of secretory and membrane proteins for insertion through the ER translocon and cleavage by signal peptidase, and a separate mitochondrial system in which bacterial-like mitoribosomes synthesise the thirteen respiratory chain subunits encoded by mtDNA. Quality-control components resolve stalled ribosomes and degrade aberrant nascent chains. Defects cause ribosomopathies, anaemias and mitochondrial disease."
