    "source": "go",
    "name": "small nucleolar ribonucleoprotein complex assembly",
    "description": "small nucleolar ribonucleoprotein complex assembly builds the box C/D and H/ACA particles that guide 2'-O-methylation and pseudouridylation of ribosomal RNA, and so determines whether a cell can make functional ribosomes at all. Assembly is not spontaneous: dedicated chaperones and assembly factors capture nascent core proteins before they meet RNA, with SHQ1 holding dyskerin and NAF1 placing it on H/ACA RNAs, while ZNHIT3, ZNHIT6 and NUFIP1 chaperone the box C/D core protein SNU13 onto its RNA. The AAA+ ATPases RUVBL1 and RUVBL2, together with PIH1D1 and the R2TP adaptor machinery, provide the energy-dependent remodelling that licenses these handovers. The same route supplies telomerase, which shares an H/ACA core. Loss of these factors impairs ribosome biogenesis and nucleolar function, and mutations in the pathway cause ribosomopathies and neurodevelopmental disease."
