   "exemplar": {
    "key": "go:GO:0006369",
    "source": "go",
    "name": "termination of RNA polymerase II transcription",
    "description": "termination of RNA polymerase II transcription is the step that tells an elongating polymerase where a gene ends, releasing the finished pre-mRNA and clearing the template so that transcription units do not run into their neighbours. Without it, read-through transcription generates chimeric and antisense RNAs, interferes with downstream promoters, and leaves stalled polymerase and RNA:DNA hybrids that threaten genome stability. Termination is coupled to 3' end processing: cleavage factors such as PCF11 recognise poly(A) signals via the polymerase C-terminal domain, the exonuclease XRN2 degrades the downstream RNA to chase the enzyme off the DNA, and the helicase senataxin resolves R-loops at pause sites. Phosphatase activity, notably SSU72 and PP1 with its regulator PNUTS, reverses CTD and Spt5 phosphorylation to convert elongation into termination, while TTF2 strips polymerase from chromatin. The pathway also shapes gene looping, transcriptional recycling and mitotic silencing."
   },
