   "exemplar": {
    "key": "go:GO:0008033",
    "source": "go",
    "name": "tRNA processing",
    "description": "tRNA processing converts newly transcribed pre-tRNAs into the mature adaptors that decode mRNA, so every round of protein synthesis depends on it. Precursors must be trimmed at both ends by the RNase P complex and by 3' endonucleases, have CCA added by a nucleotidyltransferase, and in the case of intron-containing tRNAs be cut by the splicing endonuclease and rejoined by an RNA ligase complex. Dozens of modification enzymes then decorate the body and anticodon loop — pseudouridine synthases, methyltransferases, dihydrouridine and queuosine enzymes, thiolation and wybutosine machinery, and the elongator and KEOPS complexes acting at the wobble position — tuning decoding accuracy, translational speed and tRNA stability. Both nuclear and mitochondrial tRNA pools are served. Loss of individual enzymes causes intellectual disability, microcephaly, mitochondrial disease and anaemia, and altered wobble modification reshapes codon-biased translation in cancer and stress responses."
   },
