    "name": "positive regulation of mRNA 3'-end processing",
    "description": "positive regulation of mRNA 3'-end processing describes factors that stimulate the cleavage and polyadenylation reactions that finish a messenger RNA, the step that sets transcript stability, export competence and translational output. Efficient 3'-end formation is also what terminates transcription, so its enhancement couples the fate of an mRNA to the release of RNA polymerase II. The nuclear cap-binding complex, NCBP1 with NCBP2, ties events at the 5' cap to distal processing, illustrating how the two ends of a nascent transcript are communicated; CDC73, a component of the polymerase-associated PAF complex, links elongation to poly(A) site cleavage; the G-quadruplex-resolving helicase DHX36 acts on structured 3' untranslated regions; and CCNB1 brings cell-cycle control to bear on transcript maturation. Together these activities determine 3' untranslated region length and thus which regulatory elements a message retains."
