    "source": "go",
    "name": "miRNA-mediated gene silencing by mRNA destabilization",
    "description": "miRNA-mediated gene silencing by mRNA destabilization is the branch of microRNA regulation in which a targeted transcript is not merely blocked from translation but degraded, shortening its half-life and permanently lowering protein output. This is how animal cells set quantitative dosage for large programs of genes at once, tuning proliferation, differentiation, stress responses and lineage identity; loss or amplification of individual microRNAs of this kind is a common feature of cancer. Mature microRNAs load into an Argonaute-containing silencing complex, base-pair with sites usually in the 3' UTR, and recruit deadenylase activity through the CCR4-NOT complex, whose CNOT6, CNOT7 and CNOT8 subunits trim the poly(A) tail and commit the mRNA to decapping and decay. The RNA helicase MOV10 assists by unwinding structured targets. Represented microRNAs include the let-7 family, miR-17~92 members, and miR-34 and miR-200 family regulators of epithelial state."
