    "name": "DNA Repair",
    "description": "DNA Repair is the collective machinery that keeps the genome intact against damage from radiation, reactive metabolites, chemical adducts and errors of replication. Its failure drives mutation accumulation, cellular senescence and cancer, and inherited defects in its components underlie Fanconi anemia, xeroderma pigmentosum, ataxia-telangiectasia and Lynch syndrome; because dividing cells have little time to repair, the same machinery determines the response to DNA-damaging chemotherapy and PARP inhibitors. The genes span all the major routes: glycosylases and APEX1 with POLB and XRCC1-LIG3 in base excision repair, MSH2/MLH1/PMS2 mismatch correction of nascent strands, XPC-DDB2 and the TFIIH helicases in nucleotide excision repair, direct reversal by MGMT and ALKBH enzymes, FANC proteins unhooking interstrand crosslinks, translesion polymerases that permit damage bypass, and BRCA1/BRCA2-RAD51 recombination versus DNA-PK-mediated end joining at double-strand breaks. Overlaid are ATM/ATR checkpoint signalling, ubiquitin, SUMO and PARP marks, chromatin remodelling, and p53-directed arrest or apoptosis."
