    "source": "hallmark",
    "name": "HALLMARK_DNA_REPAIR",
    "description": "DNA repair covers the surveillance and correction of DNA lesions that would otherwise become mutations, and the transcriptional and nucleotide-supply machinery that supports it. Its failure underlies xeroderma pigmentosum, Cockayne syndrome and the mutator phenotypes of cancer. The core here is nucleotide excision repair: XPC and DDB2 recognise helix-distorting damage, the TFIIH helicases ERCC2/ERCC3 open the duplex, ERCC1-ERCC4 and ERCC5 incise it, and replicative polymerases with PCNA, RFC and LIG1 fill and seal the gap. Base excision repair (MPG, POLB, FEN1), translesion synthesis (POLH, REV3L) and homologous recombination (RAD51, RAD52, RPA) handle other lesion classes, with TP53 coordinating the damage response. The large complement of RNA polymerase subunits, general transcription factors and elongation regulators reflects transcription-coupled repair, while nucleotide kinases and salvage enzymes maintain the dNTP pools that repair synthesis consumes."
