    "source": "go",
    "name": "viral process",
    "description": "Viral process covers the whole of the host-virus interface: the host molecules a virus exploits to enter, replicate and leave a cell, and the countermeasures the cell mounts against it. It matters because these proteins set susceptibility to infection and determine whether an exposure becomes disease, and they are the targets of antiviral drugs and vaccines. Entry receptors and priming proteases such as ACE2, CD4, CCR5, NPC1, TMPRSS2, furin and the cathepsins define tropism; endosomal trafficking, glycosylation and ER-associated machinery support virion maturation; translation initiation factors, RNA-binding proteins and nuclear import carriers are commandeered for viral gene expression; and the ESCRT complexes are hijacked for budding and membrane scission. Opposing this are intrinsic and interferon-induced restriction factors -- APOBEC3 deaminases, TRIM proteins, IFITMs, tetherin, the OAS-RNase L arm and autophagy components -- which degrade viral nucleic acid, trap particles and trigger innate signalling."
