    "description": "Interactions of Vpr with host cellular proteins describes how the HIV-1 accessory protein Vpr subverts host machinery to help the virus replicate in non-dividing cells and to reshape the infected cell's fate. Its main partners here are the nuclear pore complex and nuclear import factors contributing to docking and translocation of the viral pre-integration complex through the nuclear envelope, alongside importin-alpha, the chromatin tethers BANF1, HMGA1 and PSIP1/LEDGF that accompany the incoming genome. A second arm engages the adenine nucleotide translocators of the inner mitochondrial membrane, promoting membrane permeabilization and release of apoptogenic factors. Vpr additionally arrests cells at the G2/M boundary by driving inhibitory Tyr15 phosphorylation of CDK1, a block that depends on PP2A and differs from the canonical DNA damage checkpoint. Together these activities link nucleocytoplasmic transport, cell cycle control and apoptosis to viral pathogenesis and CD4 T cell loss."
