    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Viral mRNA Translation",
    "description": "Viral mRNA Translation is the step at which influenza hands its transcripts to the host protein synthesis machinery, converting viral genetic information into the ten or more proteins needed to build progeny virions. Because the virus encodes no ribosome of its own, this dependence on host 40S and 60S subunits makes translation the point where infection is most exposed to antiviral defence and to therapeutic interference. Conserved viral 5' untranslated regions favour ribosome recruitment, assisted by the cellular RNA-binding protein GRSF1, while NS1 together with the host chaperone P58(IPK) keeps the stress kinase PKR inactive so that eIF2alpha is not phosphorylated and initiation continues. Host mRNA translation is simultaneously suppressed, redirecting ribosomes toward viral messages. The PB1 segment additionally yields PB1-F2 from an alternative reading frame, expanding the coding capacity of a compact genome."
