    "name": "Defective SLC36A2 causes iminoglycinuria (IG) and hyperglycinuria (HG)",
    "description": "Defective SLC36A2 causes iminoglycinuria (IG) and hyperglycinuria (HG) describes the loss of a kidney amino acid reabsorption step, the event that lets glycine and the imino acids proline and hydroxyproline escape into urine. SLC36A2 encodes the proton-coupled amino acid transporter PAT2, which uses the inward proton gradient of the proximal tubule brush border to recover filtered glycine and proline with high affinity; it is also expressed in muscle. When the transporter is inactivated, reabsorption fails and patients present with iminoglycinuria, an autosomal recessive trait, or the milder hyperglycinuria in which only glycine is lost. Penetrance and the exact urinary profile depend on the accompanying activity of related tubular transporters SLC6A18, SLC6A19 and SLC6A20, so the phenotype behaves as a polygenic threshold rather than a simple single-gene defect. Both conditions are generally benign, illustrating the redundancy of renal amino acid transport."
