    "description": "Diseases of glycosylation collects the inherited defects that disrupt attachment and elaboration of sugar chains on proteins and lipids, the congenital disorders of glycosylation. Because nearly every secreted and membrane protein is glycosylated, these defects produce multisystem disease, most often psychomotor retardation, seizures, coagulopathy and skeletal or connective tissue abnormality; N-glycosylation defects such as PMM2-CDG are the largest group and are detected by abnormal transferrin isoforms. The genes trace each affected step: nucleotide-sugar supply and galactose metabolism, dolichol synthesis and the ALG enzymes that build the lipid-linked precursor, oligosaccharyltransferase-associated processing, O-mannosylation of dystroglycan underlying muscular dystrophies, O-fucosylation that tunes Notch, and assembly of proteoglycan linkage regions on syndecans, glypicans and lecticans. Lysosomal glycan degradation defects and mucin-type O-glycosylation belong here too. Treatment remains largely supportive."
