    "name": "Loss-of-function mutations in DBT cause MSUD2",
    "description": "Loss-of-function mutations in DBT cause MSUD2 describes how defects in the dihydrolipoamide branched-chain transacylase subunit cripple the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex and produce maple syrup urine disease type 2. This complex performs the committed, irreversible step of leucine, isoleucine and valine catabolism, oxidatively decarboxylating their transaminated keto acids so that the carbon skeletons can enter the TCA cycle. DBT forms the E2 core to which the E1 heterotetramer of BCKDHA and BCKDHB and the shared E3 dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase attach, and its lipoyl domain shuttles the acyl group between them; losing it destabilises the entire assembly. Branched-chain amino acids and their keto acids then accumulate in blood and brain, causing the characteristic sweet-smelling urine, encephalopathy, ketoacidotic crises and, untreated, neurological damage or death. It illustrates how one scaffold subunit determines the activity of a whole multienzyme complex."
