    "name": "Impaired BRCA2 binding to RAD51",
    "description": "Impaired BRCA2 binding to RAD51 describes what happens to homologous recombination repair of DNA double-strand breaks when BRCA2 can no longer deliver RAD51 to resected DNA. BRCA2 normally uses its BRC repeats and a C-terminal TR2 site to nucleate RAD51 filaments on RPA-coated single-stranded DNA, the step that licenses strand invasion of a sister chromatid; cancer-associated mutations in these contact points leave the upstream machinery intact but the repair reaction unfinished. The accompanying genes trace that upstream arm: MRE11-RAD50-NBN with CtIP and BLM/DNA2/EXO1 carry out end resection, RPA coats the resulting overhang, and ATM, ATR-ATRIP, TOPBP1 and the 9-1-1 clamp signal the damage and arrest the cycle. Loss of the final loading step forces breaks into mutagenic alternative repair, driving genomic instability, hereditary breast and ovarian cancer predisposition, and sensitivity to PARP inhibition."
