    "description": "Defective CYP27B1 causes VDDR1B describes the loss of vitamin D activation that produces vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1B, a hereditary failure to make the active hormone despite adequate vitamin D intake or sun exposure. Cholecalciferol made in skin is itself inert; it must be hydroxylated at carbon 25 in the liver by the microsomal cytochrome P450 CYP2R1 to give calcidiol, and then at the 1-alpha position to give calcitriol, the ligand for the nuclear vitamin D receptor. Through that receptor calcitriol drives intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption, bone mineralisation, and programmes of cellular differentiation and proliferation. When the 25-hydroxylase step is crippled by mutation, circulating calcidiol is low, hormone-dependent gene expression is not engaged, and children develop hypocalcaemia and the skeletal deformities of rickets, correctable by supplying the hydroxylated metabolite."
