    "source": "go",
    "name": "GTP metabolic process",
    "description": "GTP metabolic process covers the reactions that make, interconvert and consume GTP, the nucleotide that fuels protein synthesis, microtubule dynamics and the entire family of regulatory GTP-binding switches. Because GTP pools are set separately from ATP pools, this chemistry determines whether translation, ribosome assembly and small-GTPase signalling can proceed at all; its failure is felt in proliferating cells first, and inhibiting the committed step is the basis of immunosuppressive therapy. Two arms are represented: de novo and salvage supply, where IMPDH1/2 control the rate-limiting oxidation of inosine monophosphate and nucleoside diphosphate kinases (NME family) transfer phosphate to GDP, and consumption by hydrolytic GTPases -- Ran in nucleocytoplasmic transport, Rab and Rho proteins in membrane traffic, and mitofusin and OPA1 in mitochondrial fusion and inner-membrane remodelling. LRRK2 links the same nucleotide chemistry to Parkinson disease."
