    "source": "go",
    "name": "chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan metabolic process",
    "description": "Chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan metabolic process builds and breaks down the sulfated sugar chains that give cartilage, bone and the extracellular matrix their compressive strength, and that pattern cell surfaces in the nervous system. Its products control growth factor availability, matrix hydration and axon guidance, and perineuronal nets built from these proteoglycans close critical periods of plasticity and block regeneration after injury. Synthesis begins with xylose transfer to a core protein serine and extension of the common linker tetrasaccharide, followed by alternating GalNAc and glucuronic acid addition by chondroitin synthases and sulfation at the 4-, 6- and 2-positions by dedicated sulfotransferases using PAPS imported into the Golgi. Epimerases divert chains toward dermatan sulfate. Lysosomal hyaluronidases, sulfatases and exoglycosidases complete turnover, and their loss causes mucopolysaccharidoses, while defects in linker assembly produce skeletal dysplasias and connective tissue fragility."
