    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "MPS II - Hunter syndrome (CS/DS degradation)",
    "description": "MPS II - Hunter syndrome (CS/DS degradation) describes the lysosomal step that fails in Hunter syndrome, an X-linked disorder of glycosaminoglycan turnover that affects mainly boys. Iduronate-2-sulfatase removes the 2-sulfate group from L-iduronate residues in dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate and heparin, and this desulfation must occur before the downstream exoglycosidases can continue stepwise degradation of the chain. When the enzyme is deficient or absent, partially degraded glycosaminoglycans accumulate in lysosomes throughout the body and spill into urine. The consequences are systemic: hepatosplenomegaly, thickened heart valves and reduced cardiac function, airway obstruction, joint stiffness and skeletal deformity, and in severe forms progressive neurological deterioration and death in childhood. Milder forms preserve cognition and allow survival into adulthood. The pathway situates lysosomal catabolism of extracellular matrix polysaccharides within normal tissue remodelling."
