    "name": "intestinal absorption",
    "description": "Intestinal absorption is the uptake of digested nutrients from the gut lumen into enterocytes and onward into blood and lymph, the step that converts food into usable substrate for the rest of the body. Its failure produces malabsorption, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, iron or folate deficiency anaemia, and failure to thrive. The genes describe the specialised absorptive enterocyte and its brush border: transporters for glucose and fructose (SLC5A1, SLC2A5), folate and anion carriers, sterol uptake and efflux machinery (NPC1L1 opposed by the ABCG5/ABCG8 heterodimer), lipases and acyltransferases that re-esterify fatty acids, and apolipoproteins that package them into chylomicrons. Iron handling by hephaestin and hepcidin, tight junction and claudin proteins that set paracellular permeability, and villin and ezrin that build the microvillar cytoskeleton complete the picture, with vitamin D receptor signalling tuning capacity to demand."
