    "source": "go",
    "name": "tricarboxylic acid transport",
    "description": "Tricarboxylic acid transport moves citrate and related tri- and dicarboxylic acids across membranes, linking the mitochondrial matrix, the cytosol, and the bloodstream. This traffic sets the supply of cytosolic citrate that is cleaved to acetyl-CoA for fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis and for protein acetylation, and it governs how much citrate the kidney reabsorbs rather than excretes. The mitochondrial citrate carrier SLC25A1 exports citrate in exchange for malate, while the sodium-coupled transporters SLC13A5 and SLC13A3 import citrate and succinate at the plasma membrane of hepatocytes, neurons, and renal tubule cells; SFXN5 handles citrate at the inner mitochondrial membrane. Loss of these activities has clear consequences: SLC25A1 deficiency causes combined D,L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria, SLC13A5 mutations cause early infantile epileptic encephalopathy, and reduced urinary citrate promotes calcium stone formation."
