    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Amino acid transport across the plasma membrane",
    "description": "Amino acid transport across the plasma membrane moves amino acids into and out of cells, and it is what allows dietary protein absorbed in the intestine to be reclaimed in the kidney proximal tubule and delivered to tissues for protein synthesis, neurotransmitter and glutathione production, and nitrogen handling. Physiologists first defined these activities as transport \"systems\" distinguished by substrate preference, pH dependence and coupling to sodium, chloride or exchange of a counter-amino acid; the carriers are members of several solute carrier families, including sodium-coupled neutral amino acid transporters, cationic transporters, proton-coupled carriers, and heteromeric exchangers built from a heavy chain such as SLC3A1 or SLC3A2 paired with a light chain. Loss of individual members causes recognisable transport diseases, including cystinuria, Hartnup disorder and lysinuric protein intolerance, while SLC7A11 and SLC7A5 link this uptake to redox balance, ferroptosis and mTORC1 nutrient sensing."
