    "name": "insulin secretion",
    "description": "Insulin secretion is the process by which pancreatic beta cells release stored insulin into the bloodstream in proportion to nutrient load, the central control point for whole-body glucose homeostasis; its failure produces type 2 diabetes and its congenital defects cause neonatal diabetes or hyperinsulinism. The genes trace the whole stimulus-secretion coupling chain: glucose entry and phosphorylation by glucokinase, mitochondrial metabolism generating ATP, closure of the KATP channel (KCNJ11/ABCC8), calcium entry, and SNARE- and Rab-dependent granule fusion via SNAP25, STX1A, RAB3A and synaptotagmins, with proinsulin processing and zinc loading through SLC30A8. Layered on top are amplifying and restraining inputs — incretin and adrenergic receptors, somatostatin, leptin, cAMP effectors and PKA/PKC — plus the beta-cell identity transcription factors PDX1, MAFA, NKX6-1 and the HNFs, circadian clock components, and inflammatory cytokines that degrade secretory competence."
