    "source": "go",
    "name": "lipophagy",
    "description": "Lipophagy is the selective autophagic breakdown of lipid droplets, delivering stored triglycerides and cholesterol esters to the lysosome for acid lipase hydrolysis and release of free fatty acids for beta-oxidation. It is a major route of mobilising lipid stores in hepatocytes and adipocytes during fasting, and its failure contributes to hepatic steatosis, lipotoxicity and insulin resistance. Cargo selection depends on droplet-surface proteins such as AUP1 and spartin, which recruit autophagic machinery to the droplet, while RAB7A directs the maturation and fusion steps that bring engulfed droplets to lysosomes. Nutrient and stress inputs tune the process: SESN2 links amino acid and oxidative stress sensing to autophagy induction, and adrenergic signalling through ADRB2 couples lipolytic hormone stimulation to droplet turnover. Serine palmitoyltransferase subunits tie sphingolipid synthesis into the regulation of lipid store consumption."
