    "name": "high-density lipoprotein particle remodeling",
    "description": "High-density lipoprotein particle remodeling is the continuous reshaping of HDL in plasma that lets it collect cholesterol from peripheral tissues and deliver it to the liver, the core of reverse cholesterol transport and a major determinant of atherosclerosis risk. Nascent discoidal particles built on apolipoprotein A-I mature as LCAT esterifies cholesterol to form a hydrophobic core, while phospholipid transfer protein fuses and redistributes surface lipids and CETP swaps cholesteryl ester for triglyceride with apoB-containing lipoproteins. Hepatic and endothelial lipases then hydrolyse HDL lipids, shrinking particles and releasing lipid-poor apoA-I for another cycle; SR-BI mediates selective uptake of the cholesteryl ester cargo by liver and steroidogenic tissue, and ABCG1 loads cholesterol from macrophages. Exchangeable apolipoproteins such as APOA2, APOC3 and APOE tune which enzymes act, linking this remodeling to triglyceride metabolism and clearance receptors."
