    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Lipophagy",
    "description": "Lipophagy is the selective autophagy of lipid droplets, the route by which cells mobilise stored triglyceride into free fatty acids for mitochondrial oxidation when nutrients are scarce. It supplies energy during fasting, keeps hepatocytes from accumulating fat, and its failure contributes to steatosis and the lipid overload seen in fatty liver disease and ageing tissues. Access to the droplet core is gated by its protein coat: perilipins PLIN2 and PLIN3 shield stored lipid, and their recognition by the chaperone HSPA8 and delivery to lysosomal degradation strips the surface so that autophagic membranes can engulf the droplet. The AMP-activated protein kinase subunits present here provide the nutrient sensor that triggers the process, coupling a falling energy charge to both autophagy initiation and lipolysis. Lipophagy therefore links autophagic membrane traffic to whole-body lipid and energy homeostasis."
