    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Protein lipoylation",
    "description": "Protein lipoylation builds the lipoyl cofactor inside mitochondria and attaches it to the handful of enzymes that cannot run without it: the E2 subunits of pyruvate, 2-oxoglutarate, 2-oxoadipate and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenases, plus the glycine cleavage system. It therefore sits upstream of oxidative energy metabolism, amino acid catabolism and one-carbon supply, and its failure produces severe lactic acidosis with combined dehydrogenase deficiency; loss of the carrier protein GCSH additionally causes hyperglycinemia and epileptic encephalopathy. Octanoyl groups from mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis, carried on the acyl carrier protein NDUFAB1, are transferred by LIPT2 onto GCSH; the radical-SAM enzyme LIAS then inserts two sulfurs using iron-sulfur clusters supplied through NFU1, with FDX1 providing electrons; LIPT1 finally relays the lipoyl group to the acceptor lysines of DLAT, DLST and DBT."
