    "name": "regulation of cholesterol biosynthetic process",
    "description": "regulation of cholesterol biosynthetic process is the control layer over sterol synthesis, setting how much cholesterol a cell makes to match what it already has. Because cholesterol is needed for membranes, steroid hormones and bile acids yet is toxic in excess, this feedback determines plasma lipid levels and underlies statin responsiveness and atherosclerosis risk. The core is sterol sensing in the endoplasmic reticulum: SCAP escorts the SREBF transcription factors to the Golgi for cleavage by site-1 and site-2 proteases, releasing factors that drive the enzymes of the mevalonate route, while INSIG1 retains the complex when sterols are abundant. Rate-limiting enzymes are also destroyed on demand by ERLIN-dependent and MARCHF6-mediated ER-associated degradation. Superimposed on this are AMPK and PKA phosphorylation, efflux transporters and apolipoproteins that alter sterol load, and a set of microRNAs that tune the transcriptional output."
