    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Ketone body metabolism",
    "description": "Ketone body metabolism is the fasting fuel switch: the liver converts surplus acetyl-CoA from fatty acid oxidation into water-soluble acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, exports them, and extrahepatic tissues burn them for energy. This spares glucose during prolonged fasting, exercise, or ketogenic diets and keeps the brain, which cannot oxidize fatty acids, supplied when carbohydrate is scarce. Synthesis runs through mitochondrial HMGCS2 and HMGCL, with BDH1 setting the acetoacetate/beta-hydroxybutyrate ratio according to mitochondrial redox state; acetone arises by spontaneous decarboxylation. Utilization requires OXCT1 (SCOT) and ACAT1 to regenerate acetyl-CoA for the TCA cycle, and because liver lacks SCOT the hepatic route is effectively one-way. Cytosolic AACS and ACSS3 route ketone carbon into lipid synthesis instead. Inherited defects in these steps cause hypoketotic hypoglycaemia or ketoacidotic crises, and uncontrolled flux underlies diabetic ketoacidosis."
