    "description": "Phospholipid biosynthetic process builds the glycerophospholipids that make up cellular membranes and the signalling lipids derived from them, so it determines membrane identity, organelle shape, and the platforms on which signalling complexes assemble. Its outputs are structural (phosphatidylcholine, phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylserine, cardiolipin for mitochondrial inner membranes) and regulatory (phosphoinositides that mark compartments for trafficking and autophagy). The genes cover glycerol-3-phosphate acylation and lysophospholipid acyltransferases that set fatty acyl composition, the CDP-choline and CDP-ethanolamine arms via choline/ethanolamine kinases and phosphotransferases, phosphatidylserine synthesis and decarboxylation, and the kinase-phosphatase pairs, including PI3Ks, PIP5Ks, PTEN and myotubularins, that interconvert phosphoinositide species. A large GPI-anchor branch attaches lipid anchors to surface proteins. Failures cause lipodystrophy, myopathy, neurodegeneration and inherited GPI-anchor deficiencies, and dysregulated phosphoinositide turnover drives oncogenic growth signalling."
