    "description": "Sphingolipid metabolism builds, remodels and degrades the sphingoid-based lipids that give membranes their structure and that double as signalling molecules controlling apoptosis, stress responses and lymphocyte trafficking. Cells depend almost entirely on de novo synthesis, beginning with serine palmitoyltransferase condensing serine and palmitoyl-CoA, followed by reduction, N-acylation by a family of ceramide synthases with distinct chain-length preferences, and desaturation to ceramide. Ceramide is then transferred from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi by CERT for conversion to sphingomyelin, or glucosylated by UGCG to seed glycosphingolipids and gangliosides. Sphingomyelinases, ceramidases and sphingosine kinases run the salvage arm, generating sphingosine 1-phosphate that is exported by transporters such as SPNS2 to establish the gradients guiding T cell egress. Lysosomal hydrolases and their saposin activators complete catabolism; their loss causes Gaucher, Fabry, Tay-Sachs and metachromatic leukodystrophy."
