   "blurb": "27 pathways from go 23, reactome 3, hallmark 1, covering 736 distinct genes. The label above is provisional: it is the three most distinctive terms in the member names, not a written theme name. No theme has been named or described.",
   "exemplar": {
    "key": "hallmark:HALLMARK_FATTY_ACID_METABOLISM",
    "source": "hallmark",
    "name": "HALLMARK_FATTY_ACID_METABOLISM",
    "description": "Fatty acid metabolism covers the breakdown and synthesis of fatty acids and the accessory reactions that feed them into cellular energy production. It supplies ATP during fasting and exercise, generates ketone bodies for the brain, and builds the acyl chains needed for membranes and signalling lipids; when it fails, as in the acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiencies, patients suffer hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy and rhabdomyolysis. Fatty acids are taken up via CD36 and binding proteins, activated by acyl-CoA synthetases, carried into mitochondria by the carnitine shuttle of CPT1A, CPT2 and SLC22A5, and cycled through beta-oxidation to acetyl-CoA, which enters the citric acid cycle or ketogenesis via HMGCS2. Peroxisomal oxidation handles very-long-chain substrates, FASN and elongases run the biosynthetic direction, and PPARA coordinates the whole programme transcriptionally with nutrient status."
   },
