    "name": "fatty acid derivative metabolic process",
    "description": "fatty acid derivative metabolic process covers the enzymatic conversion of fatty acids into the activated, elongated, oxygenated and esterified forms that cells actually use, rather than the core beta-oxidation of fat for fuel. It supplies the very-long-chain and ether lipids of myelin and skin barrier, the wax and diacylglycerol esters of sebum, the ketone bodies that sustain brain and heart during fasting, and the eicosanoid mediators of inflammation. Long-chain acyl-CoA synthetases and transporters activate substrates; ELOVL enzymes with the HACD/TECR reductase steps extend chains; lipoxygenases and CYP4 omega-hydroxylases oxidise them to leukotrienes and hydroxy acids; HMGCS2 and OXCT1 run ketogenesis and ketolysis; DGAT and AWAT enzymes complete esterification. Loss of individual steps causes peroxisomal disease such as X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, ichthyosis and retinal degeneration, linking this metabolism to membrane structure, barrier function and immune signalling."
