    "source": "go",
    "name": "diterpenoid metabolic process",
    "description": "diterpenoid metabolic process is, in human cells, essentially retinoid metabolism: the uptake of dietary carotenoids and vitamin A, their conversion to retinol, retinaldehyde and retinoic acid, and the disposal of excess. It supplies the visual chromophore that makes photoreception possible and the nuclear receptor ligand that patterns embryos and maintains epithelial differentiation, so its failure causes night blindness, retinal degeneration and developmental malformation. Carotenoid cleavage enzymes and lipases release retinol from esters, binding proteins and transthyretin carry it through blood and cytosol, and reversible dehydrogenase and reductase steps interconvert retinol and retinaldehyde before aldehyde dehydrogenases commit it to retinoic acid. In the eye, an esterification and isomerohydrolase cycle regenerates 11-cis-retinal for opsin. Dedicated CYP26 enzymes, other cytochromes P450 and glucuronosyltransferases then inactivate retinoic acid, keeping its concentration within the narrow range that gene regulation requires."
