    "source": "go",
    "name": "negative regulation of activin receptor signaling pathway",
    "description": "negative regulation of activin receptor signaling pathway restrains signalling through activin type I and II receptors and the SMAD2/3 branch of the TGF-beta superfamily, setting how much activin output a cell receives. This braking is what allows graded mesoderm patterning in the embryo, controls pituitary FSH synthesis, and limits activin-driven growth suppression and fibrosis in adult tissues. Restraint acts at every level: secreted follistatin and FSTL3 sequester activin dimers, inhibin alpha pairs with beta subunits to form antagonistic inhibin and competes for receptors with betaglycan, and cerberus and DAND5 block ligand in the node. At the membrane FKBP1A guards the type I receptor from premature phosphorylation, while inhibitory SMAD6/7 recruit SMURF1 to ubiquitinate receptors and SMADs, and nuclear SKI and LEMD3 silence SMAD-dependent transcription. Loss of these controls skews reproductive endocrine output and developmental axis formation."
