    "name": "negative regulation of striated muscle contraction",
    "description": "negative regulation of striated muscle contraction covers the braking mechanisms that limit how forcefully and how often skeletal and cardiac muscle contract. Relaxation is as much an active process as contraction: without it, cardiac output falls because chambers cannot refill, and skeletal muscle stiffens or fatigues. The genes point to the main routes of restraint. Calcium is cleared from the cytosol by the sarcoplasmic reticulum pump ATP2A1 with sorcin modulating release, while ATP1A2 maintains the ion gradients that set excitability. Signalling arms dampen contractile drive through beta-adrenergic receptor desensitisation by GRK2 and through phosphoinositide 3-kinase gamma acting on cyclic nucleotide compartments. Membrane organisation by BIN1 shapes the T-tubules where excitation-contraction coupling occurs. Loss of these controls contributes to diastolic dysfunction, arrhythmia and myopathy."
