    "description": "negative regulation of lipid biosynthetic process is the braking system on fat, sterol and sphingolipid production, holding synthesis in check when supply is sufficient so that cells do not accumulate toxic lipid loads. Its failure underlies hypercholesterolaemia, hepatic steatosis and insulin resistance. The core sterol-sensing arm works through INSIG1/INSIG2 retaining SCAP-SREBP in the ER and through MARCHF6- and ERLIN-mediated degradation of HMG-CoA reductase, while bile acid feedback via FGF19 and CYP7A1 limits sterol turnover. Energy sensing by AMPK and SIRT1, ubiquitin ligases such as FBXW7 and COP1 acting on lipogenic transcription factors, and ORMDL proteins restraining sphingolipid synthesis add parallel restraints. Secreted apolipoproteins and ANGPTL4 feed back on lipid handling, and a large set of microRNAs, notably miR-33a paired with SREBF, tunes lipogenic transcripts post-transcriptionally, linking this control to whole-body energy balance."
