    "description": "negative regulation of vascular endothelial cell proliferation is the braking side of blood vessel growth: the set of signals that hold endothelial cells quiescent so that vessels stop expanding once a bed is adequately perfused. It matters because unrestrained endothelial division drives tumour angiogenesis, proliferative retinopathy and intimal lesions, while excessive braking impairs wound healing and collateral vessel formation. Restraint is imposed at several levels. A soluble decoy receptor for VEGF sequesters the principal endothelial mitogen, a basement membrane collagen fragment (tumstatin) signals anti-proliferative arrest from the matrix, and transcriptional regulators such as MEF2C and PPARG tune the endothelial growth program. A large complement of microRNAs, including the endothelial-enriched miR-126 and miR-34a, miR-15b and the miR-424/503 cluster, damp growth factor and cell cycle transcripts post-transcriptionally, coupling vessel quiescence to inflammatory and metabolic cues."
