    "description": "negative regulation of DNA metabolic process collects the brakes on DNA transactions — the mechanisms that limit replication, recombination, repair and telomere elongation so that genome maintenance does not itself become a source of instability. It matters because unrestrained activity is as dangerous as too little: re-replication, illegitimate recombination, or runaway telomerase all produce chromosomal rearrangements and cancer. The genes describe several distinct restraints. Licensing inhibitors such as geminin and CDT1 antagonists prevent origins from firing twice per cycle, while p53 and CDKN1A halt replication after damage. Shelterin components and the CST complex cap chromosome ends and block nuclease and telomerase access. The shieldin–RIF1–53BP1 module suppresses resection to steer double-strand breaks away from homologous recombination, and helicases and structure-specific nucleases dissolve or restrain recombination intermediates. Linker histones and chromatin modifiers add a further layer by limiting access to DNA itself."
