    "source": "reactome",
    "name": "Striated Muscle Contraction",
    "description": "Striated Muscle Contraction is the force-generating cycle of skeletal and cardiac muscle, converting chemical energy into shortening of the sarcomere and thus into movement, posture and the pumping of blood. A rise in cytosolic calcium is read by the troponin complex (TNNC, TNNI, TNNT), which shifts tropomyosin off the actin filament and uncovers myosin binding sites; myosin heads then bind, pivot, release ADP and rebind ATP, ratcheting thin and thick filaments past one another. Titin, nebulin, tropomodulins, alpha-actinin and telethonin set filament length and anchor the lattice at the Z-disc, while dystrophin and desmin transmit force to the membrane and cytoskeleton. Because the geometry is so exacting, mutations in these components cause hypertrophic and dilated cardiomyopathies, nemaline myopathy, distal arthrogryposis and muscular dystrophy, and the isoform composition here distinguishes cardiac, fast and slow fibre types."
