    "description": "Smooth muscle contraction is the force-generating program of the involuntary muscle that lines blood vessels, airways, gut, bladder and uterus, setting vascular tone, peristalsis, bronchial calibre and continence. Its dysregulation underlies hypertension, asthma, vasospasm and intestinal dysmotility. Force comes from actin and a high ratio of myosin rather than sarcomeres, with contraction switched on by myosin light chain kinase acting on smooth muscle myosin, and switched off by nitric oxide-cGMP signalling through soluble guanylate cyclase and protein kinase G. Sensitivity to calcium is tuned by RhoA-ROCK and phosphatase inhibition, while calcium entry, extrusion pumps, sodium-calcium exchange and BK channels shape the underlying calcium and membrane-potential cycles. A broad set of adrenergic, muscarinic, endothelin, tachykinin, purinergic and prostanoid receptors provides the inputs, and myocardin with serum response factor and microRNAs such as miR-143/145 maintain the differentiated contractile phenotype."
