    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of cardiac muscle cell action potential",
    "description": "regulation of cardiac muscle cell action potential tunes the electrical impulse of heart muscle cells — how readily it fires, how fast it spreads, and how long each beat's depolarisation lasts. This control sets heart rate and the coordinated sequence of atrial and ventricular contraction; when it fails the result is long QT syndrome, sick sinus and conduction disease, catastrophic arrhythmia and sudden death. The genes span the machinery that shapes the waveform: L-type calcium entry through CACNA1C with calmodulin and CaMKII feedback, ryanodine receptor and SERCA-mediated calcium cycling, HCN channels generating the pacemaker current of the sinoatrial node, and TRPM4 background conductance. Others govern where channels sit and how excitation passes between cells — ankyrin-B and AKAP9 scaffolding, caveolins and BIN1 membrane organisation, connexin-40 gap junctions, and desmosomal proteins whose loss underlies arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy."
