    "source": "go",
    "name": "regulation of membrane depolarization",
    "description": "Regulation of membrane depolarization governs how readily an excitable cell moves its membrane potential away from rest toward the threshold for firing, setting heart rate, neuronal excitability and the timing of action potentials. Its failure produces arrhythmia and seizure: mutations in the cardiac sodium channel and its accessory beta subunits, or in their trafficking and anchoring partners such as ankyrin-G, RANGRF and SLMAP, underlie Brugada and long QT syndromes, while the HCN family carries the pacemaker current that drives spontaneous depolarization in sinoatrial node and rhythmically active neurons. Control is exerted at several levels — channel abundance and surface delivery through NEDD4L-dependent ubiquitination, phosphorylation by CaMKII and Src, calcium- and ATP-gated conductances such as TRPM4 and P2RX7, gap junction coupling, and transcriptional programs involving TBX5 and CREB1. Mitochondrial depolarization and permeability regulators link the same term to apoptotic commitment."
