    "source": "go",
    "name": "atrial cardiac muscle tissue development",
    "description": "Atrial cardiac muscle tissue development builds the thin-walled upper chambers of the heart and gives them an identity distinct from the ventricles, including the specialized conduction tissue that sets heart rate. It determines atrial wall growth, left-right asymmetry of the atria, and the formation of the sinoatrial node; its disruption underlies atrial septal defects, atrial fibrillation, sick sinus syndrome and laterality defects such as heterotaxy. Cardiac progenitors marked by MESP1 and ISL1 are directed by NKX2-5, GATA6, TBX5 and PITX2, with PITX2 enforcing left atrial character and TBX3/SHOX2 repressing working-myocardial genes to carve out pacemaker tissue expressing HCN4. BMP10 and BMP4, tuned by the antagonist noggin and by endoglin, drive chamber myocardial proliferation, while MYH6 and gap junction and Popeye-domain proteins provide contractile and electrical coupling. The pathway sits within broader cardiac morphogenesis and cardiac conduction."
